WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:08.850
- As you come in, I'll
start to welcome you.

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:09.992
I'm Paul North.

00:00:09.992 --> 00:00:13.732
And these are the Franke
lectures in the Humanities

00:00:13.732 --> 00:00:16.130
at the Whitney Humanities Center.

00:00:16.130 --> 00:00:19.090
This is a series called the
value of Marx's capital,

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:22.060
which I'm convening along
with my co-conspirator,

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:26.389
Paul Reitter at Ohio State
who is the valiant translator

00:00:26.389 --> 00:00:31.389
of a new translation of
Volume I into English.

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:34.330
The series is also a companion

00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:36.267
to an undergraduate seminar.

00:00:36.267 --> 00:00:41.000
So the undergrads have
valiantly gone through Volume I,

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.350
with a kind of a microscope

00:00:43.326 --> 00:00:45.472
and they've survived and
if they have survived,

00:00:45.472 --> 00:00:48.120
they're in the audience tonight.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:49.520
I have to read this.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:50.657
The Franke lectures are made possible

00:00:50.657 --> 00:00:53.930
by the generosity of
Richard and Barbara Franke,

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:54.763
and are intended to present

00:00:54.763 --> 00:00:56.700
important topics in the humanities

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:58.589
to a wide and general audience.

00:00:58.589 --> 00:01:00.240
I need to thank as I always do,

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:03.393
Alice Kaplan, the director of
the Whitney Humanities Center,

00:01:03.393 --> 00:01:06.891
Sandra Malan-Bowles and
Leana Hirschfeld-Kroen

00:01:06.891 --> 00:01:09.640
who have done behind the scenes organizing

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:13.630
and Audrey Leak who is our tech genius,

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:16.443
making things stay on track.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:19.060
I know a lot of you have been here.

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:20.153
So you've heard this before.

00:01:20.153 --> 00:01:22.800
We will have the presentation
from our speaker.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.561
Then, tonight we're going
to have a short response

00:01:25.561 --> 00:01:29.799
by our friend and another co-conspirator

00:01:29.799 --> 00:01:32.970
Michael Heinrich, who's back to discuss

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:35.470
a little bit with Fred Moseley,

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:36.470
and then we'll open to questions,

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:39.640
hold your questions and
put them into the chat.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:41.010
When we get to that period,

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:42.567
otherwise, I will lose them.

00:01:42.567 --> 00:01:47.083
I'm doing my best to
keep everybody in order.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:50.549
It is a great pleasure
to introduce Fred Moseley

00:01:50.549 --> 00:01:53.210
who is an emeritus professor of economics

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:55.300
at Mount Holyoke College,

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:59.560
where he taught Marx and
Marxian economic theory

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:00.650
for many years.

00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:03.250
He has published extensively
on Marxian theory

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:07.060
and the Marxian analysis
of the US economy.

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:09.080
His latest book is "Money and Totality,"

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:12.894
a macromonetary interpretation
of Marx's logic in "Capital."

00:02:12.894 --> 00:02:15.124
And the end of the transformation problem,

00:02:15.124 --> 00:02:18.263
we can celebrate that that was from 2016.

00:02:18.263 --> 00:02:20.300
He also edited and wrote

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.370
the introduction to
the English translation

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:26.351
of Marx's economic
manuscript of 1864 to 65.

00:02:26.351 --> 00:02:27.910
These are the manuscripts that

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:29.580
are newly translated into English

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:34.580
that became Volume III of
"Capital," edited by Engels.

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:36.250
He's a longtime member

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:38.076
of the Union for Radical
Political Economics,

00:02:38.076 --> 00:02:40.300
and served as program director

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:42.191
for the URPE sessions at the annual

00:02:42.191 --> 00:02:44.728
convention of the American
Economic Association

00:02:44.728 --> 00:02:46.393
for 10 years.

00:02:46.393 --> 00:02:48.287
I'd like to welcome Fred Moseley.

00:02:48.287 --> 00:02:52.593
Fred, I'm gonna give the
microphone over to you.

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:56.480
- Well, thank you very much, Paul.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:59.490
And thank you for the invitation

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:02.750
to participate in this very

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:08.153
interesting and innovative,
I think, seminar.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:12.845
I've watched some of the other sessions

00:03:12.845 --> 00:03:17.845
and it's been really an inspiration

00:03:20.265 --> 00:03:24.800
to hear the different
perspectives on Volume I.

00:03:26.306 --> 00:03:31.306
Thanks also to the Whitney Foundation

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:34.153
for the generous funding for this seminar

00:03:34.153 --> 00:03:39.153
that is really quite a contribution.

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:44.810
Both the theory and the
pedagogy, I think Marxian theory.

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:52.723
Well, the paper I'm gonna present tonight

00:03:52.723 --> 00:03:56.040
is different from my original intention

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.220
and the one I sent to Paul in September

00:03:58.220 --> 00:03:59.205
just to explain a little bit.

00:03:59.205 --> 00:04:02.925
That paper was an overview

00:04:02.925 --> 00:04:04.607
of all of Volume I

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:09.314
with a focus on these
two important aspects

00:04:09.314 --> 00:04:12.294
that I discussed in my recent book,

00:04:12.294 --> 00:04:17.294
The monetary nature of
Marxist theory in Volume I,

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:21.720
and the macro nature of Volume I,

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:25.543
mainly explaining the total surplus value

00:04:25.543 --> 00:04:27.863
and the economy as a whole.

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:32.620
However, listing to the presentation

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:34.873
by my old friend, Michael Heinrich,

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:39.250
in his presentation in the seminar,

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:44.250
I thought I would add a
few comments to my paper,

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:49.540
and then I kept adding
more and more comments,

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:52.010
and eventually I had a whole new paper,

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:56.639
obviously very stimulated
by Michael's opportunity

00:04:56.639 --> 00:04:58.840
to engage again with Michael

00:05:00.009 --> 00:05:02.259
and it decided I would
discuss this new paper

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:06.613
in my presentation, both because

00:05:06.613 --> 00:05:09.450
that's what I'm most
interested in at the moment

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:14.450
and I also hope you
will be more interesting

00:05:14.455 --> 00:05:16.236
to participants to dig deep

00:05:16.236 --> 00:05:19.403
into the all important chapter one,

00:05:19.403 --> 00:05:23.080
and to consider these two
somewhat different interpretations

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:26.800
of this important chapter.

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:29.292
Some of you may have read the other paper

00:05:29.292 --> 00:05:31.130
and have questions about that,

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:33.623
I would of course be happy to discuss.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:37.630
And let me say, even though Michael

00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:42.610
and I disagree about some aspects

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:47.610
of interpretation of Marx's
theory of value and money,

00:05:47.890 --> 00:05:50.260
in chapter one, I wanna say at the outset

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:54.790
how much I appreciate
Michael's contribution

00:05:55.810 --> 00:06:00.230
to Marxian scholarship and how
much I have learned from him

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:02.040
especially about the MEGA.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:03.617
I first learned about the MEGA

00:06:03.617 --> 00:06:06.725
from Michael in the early 90s

00:06:06.725 --> 00:06:11.725
from a 1989 paper he
published in Capital and Class

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:16.510
about the manuscript of 1861, 63,

00:06:16.510 --> 00:06:19.660
published for the first
time in the MEGA in the 80s,

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:21.740
and almost unknown at the time,

00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:23.830
among English speaking Marxist

00:06:23.830 --> 00:06:26.620
and I was very excited, including myself.

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:28.740
I was very excited to learn about MEGA.

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:30.671
And much of my research since then,

00:06:30.671 --> 00:06:35.671
has been related to the
MEGA in one way or another.

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:40.862
So for me, and for many
other English speaking

00:06:40.862 --> 00:06:45.010
and German challenged Marxist,

00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:48.860
Michael, has been a very important kind

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:53.443
of conduits from the
German Marxian scholarship,

00:06:53.443 --> 00:06:58.443
and especially the MEGA to Anglo Marxist,

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:00.804
which I appreciate very much.

00:07:00.804 --> 00:07:03.095
You could say today, maybe Michael

00:07:03.095 --> 00:07:05.900
has been a sort of super spread

00:07:09.377 --> 00:07:13.530
in a good sense of the term
of German Marxian scholarship.

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:19.480
So my talk will focus on chapter one,

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:23.740
then, by all accounts chapter one

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:25.530
is a very difficult chapter.

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:29.400
And for that reason, very controversial.

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:30.233
Yeah.

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:33.940
Marx said in the preface
to the first edition,

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:35.810
if you remember,

00:07:35.810 --> 00:07:39.870
beginnings are always
difficult in all sciences.

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:41.590
The understanding of the first chapter

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:44.455
will therefore present
the greatest difficulty.

00:07:44.455 --> 00:07:47.397
"I have popularized," he says,

00:07:47.397 --> 00:07:50.249
"the passages concerning
the substance of value

00:07:50.249 --> 00:07:53.807
"and the magnitude of
value as much as possible.

00:07:53.807 --> 00:07:57.323
"The section and the form of
value is the most difficult.

00:07:58.259 --> 00:08:01.247
"My favorite preference,
actually, the Volume I

00:08:01.247 --> 00:08:02.590
"is to the French edition."

00:08:02.590 --> 00:08:04.913
I don't know if you've
noticed this or not.

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:10.187
But he says, "the method of
analysis which I have employed,

00:08:10.187 --> 00:08:12.537
"and which had not previously been applied

00:08:12.537 --> 00:08:14.327
"to economic subjects,

00:08:14.327 --> 00:08:18.207
"makes the reading of the
first chapter rather arduous.

00:08:18.207 --> 00:08:21.298
"And it is to be feared
that the French public

00:08:21.298 --> 00:08:24.527
"substitute the American public,

00:08:24.527 --> 00:08:26.917
"always impatient to come to a conclusion,

00:08:26.917 --> 00:08:28.277
"eager to know the connection

00:08:28.277 --> 00:08:31.677
"between the general principles
and the immediate questions

00:08:31.677 --> 00:08:33.209
"that have aroused their passions

00:08:33.209 --> 00:08:38.209
"may be disheartened because
they cannot move on at once."

00:08:40.810 --> 00:08:42.417
Marx says "this is a disadvantage

00:08:42.417 --> 00:08:46.291
"I am powerless to overcome,
unless it be by for warning

00:08:46.291 --> 00:08:51.291
"and for arming those readers
who zealously seek the truth.

00:08:52.407 --> 00:08:55.027
"There's no royal road to science,

00:08:55.027 --> 00:08:57.247
"no easy road to science,

00:08:57.247 --> 00:08:58.812
"and only those who do not dread

00:08:58.812 --> 00:09:02.567
"the fatiguing climb of its steep paths

00:09:02.567 --> 00:09:05.230
"have a chance of gaining
its luminous (indistinct)."

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:08.554
So you probably have found

00:09:08.554 --> 00:09:12.423
chapter one to be a fatiguing climb.

00:09:13.910 --> 00:09:18.253
And I hope there will also
be some looming assignments.

00:09:21.430 --> 00:09:23.202
So first, I wanna briefly describe

00:09:23.202 --> 00:09:28.202
the overall logical
structure of chapter one

00:09:28.690 --> 00:09:31.680
that Marx alluded to in these prefaces.

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:34.650
And the key point to understand

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:38.490
about the logical structure

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:41.170
is that there are three main aspects

00:09:42.340 --> 00:09:45.486
of Marx's concepts of value.

00:09:45.486 --> 00:09:48.224
It's a complicated concept, right?

00:09:48.224 --> 00:09:53.224
And these three aspects
are substance of value,

00:09:53.490 --> 00:09:57.993
the magnitude of value and
the form of appearance.

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:04.763
Value and so, I will briefly
describe each one of these.

00:10:06.700 --> 00:10:09.508
First the substance of value, right?

00:10:09.508 --> 00:10:13.180
This is the common
property of commodities,

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:15.240
that determines their exchange values

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:17.020
with other commodities,

00:10:17.020 --> 00:10:18.490
which Marx of course assumed

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:23.490
to be the abstract labor
required to produce commodities.

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:25.520
So, that's the substance of value,

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:27.398
the common property that determines

00:10:27.398 --> 00:10:28.973
the exchange value.

00:10:30.820 --> 00:10:31.653
Right?

00:10:31.653 --> 00:10:34.642
Then the magnitude of value
is quantitative, right?

00:10:34.642 --> 00:10:39.642
The quantity of the
substance of value required

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:42.650
to produce commodities,

00:10:42.650 --> 00:10:45.710
the quantity of abstract labor required,

00:10:45.710 --> 00:10:48.510
contained in commodities which Marx called

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:50.940
the socially necessary labor time

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:55.520
contained in commodity, right?

00:10:55.520 --> 00:11:00.520
So substance magnitude then the
form of appearance of value,

00:11:00.950 --> 00:11:05.113
the money and prices, the
observable phenomena, right?

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:08.587
The substance and magnitude of value

00:11:08.587 --> 00:11:11.641
are not directly observable as such,

00:11:11.641 --> 00:11:15.230
in units of abstract labor,

00:11:15.230 --> 00:11:19.610
therefore, commodities
require an observable form

00:11:19.610 --> 00:11:23.090
of appearance, so that the magnitude

00:11:23.090 --> 00:11:25.350
of each commodity can be compared

00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:28.420
and equated with the magnitudes of value

00:11:28.420 --> 00:11:29.360
of all other commodities.

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:32.311
And this observable form of appearance

00:11:32.311 --> 00:11:37.311
is ultimately derived as
money and money prices.

00:11:39.700 --> 00:11:42.180
Sections one and two of chapter one

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:45.430
are about the substance
and magnitude of value,

00:11:45.430 --> 00:11:47.250
section one is the most important.

00:11:47.250 --> 00:11:51.230
The title of section one
is substance of value,

00:11:51.230 --> 00:11:55.990
the subtitle, substance of
value, magnitude of value, right?

00:11:55.990 --> 00:11:58.690
In section three is about
the form of appearance

00:12:00.452 --> 00:12:04.507
of value, its title is the
value form, or exchange.

00:12:06.250 --> 00:12:09.150
The part I wanna emphasize is that there

00:12:09.150 --> 00:12:12.700
is a strict causal relation

00:12:12.700 --> 00:12:16.793
between these three aspects of value.

00:12:17.960 --> 00:12:20.134
Causal relation is that the substance

00:12:20.134 --> 00:12:25.134
and the magnitude of value
are derived and presupposed

00:12:25.641 --> 00:12:28.960
in section one, one and two,

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:31.542
and then the form of appearance of value

00:12:31.542 --> 00:12:35.850
is derived from the
substance and magnitude

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:38.440
of value in volume three.

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:40.570
So I think it really helps us

00:12:40.570 --> 00:12:43.020
to understand this
overall logical structure.

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:45.369
And then the whole chapter I think,

00:12:45.369 --> 00:12:48.120
becomes more intelligible

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:52.370
and perhaps less of a fatiguing climb,

00:12:52.370 --> 00:12:56.153
excuse me, my voice isn't
what it used to be at 19.

00:13:03.540 --> 00:13:07.553
Okay, so then one more
kind of introduction,

00:13:08.410 --> 00:13:13.410
just to kind of preview and
identify four important,

00:13:15.222 --> 00:13:20.222
controversial issues,
involved in chapter one,

00:13:23.180 --> 00:13:26.540
in the interpretation of chapter one.

00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:29.778
So the first issue is in section one,

00:13:29.778 --> 00:13:33.117
Marx's derivation of abstract labor,

00:13:33.117 --> 00:13:36.100
as the substance of value

00:13:36.100 --> 00:13:39.840
as the common property of commodities,

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:42.660
that determines their exchange values.

00:13:42.660 --> 00:13:47.660
And the issue is, what's the
logic of this derivation?

00:13:47.910 --> 00:13:50.590
And is the derivation value acceptable?

00:13:52.480 --> 00:13:57.480
Second issue with respect
to the magnitude of value,

00:13:58.100 --> 00:13:59.970
defined in Volume I,

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:03.320
is this magnitude of value of property

00:14:03.320 --> 00:14:06.313
of each single individual commodity,

00:14:07.637 --> 00:14:10.990
or is the magnitude of value a property

00:14:10.990 --> 00:14:14.093
of multiple commodities
possessed jointly together?

00:14:15.580 --> 00:14:20.340
Actually, I learned from an
email from Michael yesterday

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:23.620
that perhaps we don't disagree as much

00:14:23.620 --> 00:14:26.660
as I previously thought about that.

00:14:26.660 --> 00:14:30.360
And so I'm happy about that

00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:33.890
and will not concentrate on that as much.

00:14:33.890 --> 00:14:35.520
Third issue.

00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:36.550
And this is really, I think,

00:14:36.550 --> 00:14:39.930
the most important issue both
between Michael and myself

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:43.053
and the general Marxian scholarship,

00:14:44.130 --> 00:14:49.053
is the magnitude of value
determined entirely in production,

00:14:50.060 --> 00:14:55.060
the production process
or does it also depend

00:14:55.370 --> 00:14:57.710
at least in part on circulation

00:14:59.350 --> 00:15:02.880
and demand for a particularly product.

00:15:04.477 --> 00:15:06.109
And unfortunately, just the derivation

00:15:06.109 --> 00:15:09.490
in section three of money and prices

00:15:09.490 --> 00:15:13.080
is the necessary form
of appearance of value.

00:15:13.080 --> 00:15:15.230
And for me, the main issue here is

00:15:15.230 --> 00:15:17.954
that this very important section

00:15:17.954 --> 00:15:22.954
is often overlooked or
not discussed in any depth

00:15:25.720 --> 00:15:29.350
and I think it's important
in part in large part

00:15:29.350 --> 00:15:31.430
because of it's difficulty and it seemed

00:15:31.430 --> 00:15:33.620
as so boring and detail,

00:15:33.620 --> 00:15:37.053
but it's very important as we shall see.

00:15:38.330 --> 00:15:43.330
So, I will now review
kind of section by section

00:15:47.060 --> 00:15:50.670
my interpretation of chapter one

00:15:52.230 --> 00:15:55.400
with substantial textual references,

00:15:55.400 --> 00:16:00.270
and then I will discuss
at least one aspect

00:16:00.270 --> 00:16:04.989
in particular of Michael's interpretation,

00:16:04.989 --> 00:16:09.989
this third issue is the determination

00:16:10.440 --> 00:16:14.093
of the magnitude of value in
production and or exchange.

00:16:19.640 --> 00:16:20.510
Okay.

00:16:20.510 --> 00:16:25.510
So, section one, the substance
of value abstract labor,

00:16:26.480 --> 00:16:30.423
the magnitude of value,
socially necessary labor time.

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:32.753
Section one begins with an analysis

00:16:32.753 --> 00:16:37.733
of a commodity as a general
system of commodity exchange,

00:16:38.610 --> 00:16:40.328
and this general system is assumed

00:16:40.328 --> 00:16:43.833
to be mutually consistent,

00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:49.820
A is equal to B, and B is equal
to C, and A is equal to C.

00:16:49.970 --> 00:16:52.979
And this property of
mutual consistency implies

00:16:52.979 --> 00:16:56.860
that commodities are treated objectively

00:16:56.860 --> 00:17:00.660
as equivalent, exchange is
the exchange of equivalents.

00:17:00.660 --> 00:17:04.200
Each commodity is in principle

00:17:04.200 --> 00:17:06.100
equivalent with all other commodities.

00:17:10.060 --> 00:17:15.060
And this relation of
equivalence in turn implies

00:17:15.520 --> 00:17:17.950
that each commodity must possess

00:17:17.950 --> 00:17:22.000
some common property and
possess this common property

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:24.556
in definite quantities that determine

00:17:24.556 --> 00:17:27.481
the ratios in which commodities

00:17:27.481 --> 00:17:30.623
are equivalent with one another.

00:17:31.510 --> 00:17:34.310
Marx discusses an example of a relation

00:17:34.310 --> 00:17:37.490
of equality between two commodities,

00:17:37.490 --> 00:17:40.005
the famous corn and iron example,

00:17:40.005 --> 00:17:44.185
which he expressed in terms
of an equation, right?

00:17:44.185 --> 00:17:49.185
Emphasizes the

00:17:50.650 --> 00:17:54.440
relation of equality between commodities,

00:17:54.440 --> 00:17:56.949
Marx says "whatever their
relation of exchange may be,

00:17:56.949 --> 00:18:01.590
"it can always be represented
by an equation," right?

00:18:01.590 --> 00:18:02.974
In which a given quantity of corn

00:18:02.974 --> 00:18:07.740
is equated with some quantity of a buyer.

00:18:07.740 --> 00:18:11.170
So, what does this equation signify?

00:18:11.170 --> 00:18:14.877
Marx says "it signifies
that a common element

00:18:14.877 --> 00:18:19.477
"of identical magnitude
exists in both of these things

00:18:19.477 --> 00:18:20.777
"in the two (indistinct)."

00:18:23.133 --> 00:18:24.702
And Marx of course argued that

00:18:24.702 --> 00:18:27.323
this common property of commodities,

00:18:27.323 --> 00:18:32.023
that determines their exchange
values is abstract labor,

00:18:33.260 --> 00:18:34.773
the substance of value.

00:18:36.080 --> 00:18:39.586
Marx summarized his
derivation of abstract labor,

00:18:39.586 --> 00:18:44.250
as the substance of value
in the following key passes.

00:18:44.250 --> 00:18:48.189
So, let us look at the residue
of the products of labor.

00:18:48.189 --> 00:18:50.620
So we're looking at a commodity here

00:18:50.620 --> 00:18:53.160
considered within a system

00:18:53.160 --> 00:18:55.500
of general commodity exchange, right?

00:18:55.500 --> 00:18:57.510
So, what do we see, well,

00:18:57.510 --> 00:18:59.120
we don't really see anything,

00:18:59.120 --> 00:19:02.421
but we know, we now understand

00:19:02.421 --> 00:19:05.029
that there is nothing left of them,

00:19:05.029 --> 00:19:09.313
but what he calls a
phantom-like objectivity.

00:19:11.700 --> 00:19:13.140
They are merely congealed

00:19:13.140 --> 00:19:16.463
quantities of homogeneous human labor.

00:19:17.630 --> 00:19:20.530
All these things now
tell us as we observe,

00:19:20.530 --> 00:19:22.920
tell us is that human labor power

00:19:22.920 --> 00:19:26.670
has been expended to produce human labor

00:19:26.670 --> 00:19:29.180
is accumulated in them,

00:19:29.180 --> 00:19:31.460
as crystals of this social substance

00:19:31.460 --> 00:19:34.023
which is common to them
all they are values.

00:19:35.530 --> 00:19:39.230
So, Marx described as we see the substance

00:19:39.230 --> 00:19:43.390
of value as a phantom-like objectivity.

00:19:43.390 --> 00:19:46.950
Because, this abstract labor
contained in commodities

00:19:46.950 --> 00:19:50.040
is not directly observable as such.

00:19:50.040 --> 00:19:53.480
It exists, it's contained
within commodities,

00:19:53.480 --> 00:19:55.968
but it's not something we can observe

00:19:55.968 --> 00:20:00.853
and we will come back to
this point in section three.

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:05.273
So, Marx's derivation then,

00:20:06.460 --> 00:20:09.300
of abstract labor as
the substance of value

00:20:09.300 --> 00:20:12.240
can consist of the following steps.

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:16.040
Each commodity is considered
to be an equal and equivalent

00:20:16.040 --> 00:20:17.192
to all other commodities,

00:20:17.192 --> 00:20:19.429
the relation of equality implies

00:20:19.429 --> 00:20:23.357
that all commodities possess
a common property, right?

00:20:23.357 --> 00:20:26.460
And this common property
is (indistinct), right?

00:20:26.460 --> 00:20:31.460
So, that's the bare bones of
the derivation in section one.

00:20:33.930 --> 00:20:37.900
And in the next paragraph,
after the summary,

00:20:37.900 --> 00:20:42.840
Marx preview, is later
derivation of section three,

00:20:42.840 --> 00:20:46.670
on money as the necessary
form of appearance

00:20:46.670 --> 00:20:50.030
of this substance of value abstract labor.

00:20:50.030 --> 00:20:55.030
And he noted, Marx noted in this preview,

00:20:55.380 --> 00:20:56.670
we must first however,

00:20:56.670 --> 00:20:58.153
we must first consider the nature

00:20:58.153 --> 00:21:02.990
of value independently of
its form of appearance.

00:21:02.990 --> 00:21:05.612
So first, we're just gonna
consider the commodity

00:21:05.612 --> 00:21:07.960
in terms of abstract labor

00:21:07.960 --> 00:21:09.633
and socially necessary labor time,

00:21:09.633 --> 00:21:13.259
independently of money and prices.

00:21:13.259 --> 00:21:17.357
And he says "the progress
of our investigation

00:21:17.357 --> 00:21:20.007
"will bring us back to exchange value

00:21:20.007 --> 00:21:22.307
"as the necessary mode of expression

00:21:22.307 --> 00:21:24.833
"form of appearance of value.

00:21:24.833 --> 00:21:26.267
"For the present, however,

00:21:26.267 --> 00:21:29.226
"we must first consider
the nature of value

00:21:29.226 --> 00:21:33.627
"independently of its form of appearance."

00:21:35.660 --> 00:21:40.571
And also, independently of
any actual act of exchange,

00:21:40.571 --> 00:21:42.310
because we're just observing

00:21:42.310 --> 00:21:44.453
this commodity and its properties.

00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:49.320
On next page that Marx define

00:21:49.320 --> 00:21:54.320
the magnitude of value in units
of labor time, hours, days,

00:21:54.950 --> 00:21:58.510
independently of its form of appearance,

00:21:58.510 --> 00:21:59.653
in terms of money.

00:22:01.060 --> 00:22:04.530
The quantity, the magnitude of value

00:22:04.530 --> 00:22:06.456
is measured by its duration,

00:22:06.456 --> 00:22:11.213
labor time and a particular
scale of hours or days.

00:22:12.900 --> 00:22:16.662
And Marx clarified that
the hours of labor time

00:22:16.662 --> 00:22:20.487
that constitute the magnitude of value

00:22:20.487 --> 00:22:25.355
are not measured by actual
hours of concrete labor,

00:22:25.355 --> 00:22:27.489
which would be observable,

00:22:27.489 --> 00:22:30.090
but are instead measured in terms

00:22:30.090 --> 00:22:33.410
of a social average, quantities of labor

00:22:33.410 --> 00:22:36.546
or what he calls socially
necessary labor time

00:22:36.546 --> 00:22:38.751
required to produce commodities.

00:22:38.751 --> 00:22:43.751
His definition in a sentence,
socially necessary labor time

00:22:43.760 --> 00:22:47.910
is the labor time required
to produce any use value

00:22:47.910 --> 00:22:50.089
under the conditions of production,

00:22:50.089 --> 00:22:52.540
normal for a given society,

00:22:52.540 --> 00:22:55.720
and with the average degree
of skill and intensity

00:22:55.720 --> 00:22:58.530
of labor prevalent in that society.

00:22:58.530 --> 00:23:03.150
So, the magnitude of
value is a social average,

00:23:03.150 --> 00:23:08.123
not any particular
individual quantity only.

00:23:09.600 --> 00:23:11.819
And then in the next
paragraph Marx summarize

00:23:11.819 --> 00:23:15.633
the very clearly what
exclusively determines

00:23:15.633 --> 00:23:19.167
the magnitude of the value of any article

00:23:19.167 --> 00:23:23.732
is therefore, the amount of
labor, socially necessary,

00:23:23.732 --> 00:23:27.763
the labor time socially
necessary for its production.

00:23:30.670 --> 00:23:33.160
So one other important
point in section one

00:23:33.160 --> 00:23:36.850
that I didn't mention in my paper,

00:23:36.850 --> 00:23:40.127
is that "socially necessary
labor time," Marx says,

00:23:40.127 --> 00:23:43.763
"varies inversely with
the productivity of labor,

00:23:44.826 --> 00:23:48.310
"I.e with the quantity of
a product produced per hour

00:23:49.227 --> 00:23:51.007
"of abstract labor,"

00:23:51.933 --> 00:23:55.563
and I will come back to
this point later as well.

00:23:58.210 --> 00:24:00.680
So toward the end of section one, look,

00:24:00.680 --> 00:24:03.970
thinking again about this
overall logical structure

00:24:03.970 --> 00:24:05.772
and the three aspects of value,

00:24:05.772 --> 00:24:09.728
toward the end, Marx summarizes
the argument thus far

00:24:09.728 --> 00:24:12.599
in another preview, of section three

00:24:12.599 --> 00:24:14.220
on the form of appearance,

00:24:14.220 --> 00:24:18.250
with the following summary

00:24:18.250 --> 00:24:20.630
of the logical structure of chapter one,

00:24:20.630 --> 00:24:23.777
he says, "now we know the
substance of value," right?

00:24:23.777 --> 00:24:25.110
"It is labor."

00:24:25.110 --> 00:24:29.193
We know the measure of its
magnitude, it is labor time.

00:24:30.860 --> 00:24:35.450
The form which stamps
value as exchange value

00:24:35.450 --> 00:24:37.890
remains to be analyzed.

00:24:37.890 --> 00:24:42.490
But before this, we need to
develop the characteristics

00:24:42.490 --> 00:24:45.431
we have already found more fully.

00:24:45.431 --> 00:24:49.760
Develop the substance
and magnitude more fully

00:24:51.317 --> 00:24:53.350
in terms of labor time,

00:24:53.350 --> 00:24:55.340
in terms of abstract labor

00:24:55.340 --> 00:24:57.006
and socially necessary labor time,

00:24:57.006 --> 00:25:00.729
before we go on to the formal appearance

00:25:00.729 --> 00:25:03.440
of value in section three.

00:25:03.440 --> 00:25:08.440
So, I think Marx is emphasizing
this logical structure,

00:25:08.538 --> 00:25:13.538
which I think is very important.

00:25:17.270 --> 00:25:18.839
Now, just a word about section two

00:25:18.839 --> 00:25:21.620
which further develops the substance

00:25:21.620 --> 00:25:25.783
and magnitude of value
prior to money and prices.

00:25:28.477 --> 00:25:32.927
And he further clarifies
that the precise meaning

00:25:32.927 --> 00:25:37.240
of the magnitude of value as
socially necessary labor time

00:25:38.458 --> 00:25:43.458
in units of hours or days is,

00:25:45.144 --> 00:25:49.050
he clarifies the assumption
about different skills

00:25:49.050 --> 00:25:51.350
and how he treats skilled labor.

00:25:51.350 --> 00:25:54.094
He states that the basic unit of measure

00:25:54.094 --> 00:25:59.060
of socially necessary labor
time is simple unskilled labor

00:25:59.060 --> 00:26:01.091
without special development.

00:26:01.091 --> 00:26:06.091
And that then skilled labor is
treated in the following way,

00:26:06.210 --> 00:26:08.070
one hour of skilled labor

00:26:08.070 --> 00:26:11.162
is assumed to count in the determination

00:26:11.162 --> 00:26:15.860
of the magnitude of value
as some of the coefficient

00:26:15.860 --> 00:26:19.510
of one hour of simple unskilled labor,

00:26:19.510 --> 00:26:22.125
Marx put it more complex labor counts

00:26:22.125 --> 00:26:26.290
as intensify or rather multiply,

00:26:26.290 --> 00:26:30.086
simple labor so that a smaller
quantity of complex labor

00:26:30.086 --> 00:26:34.250
is considered equal to a larger quantity

00:26:34.250 --> 00:26:37.072
of simple labor, right?

00:26:37.072 --> 00:26:42.072
And this reduction is
assumed to have taken place

00:26:43.680 --> 00:26:46.370
here in section two, right?

00:26:46.370 --> 00:26:51.370
Prior to the derivation of
money in the form of appearance

00:26:52.470 --> 00:26:54.733
in section three.

00:26:56.410 --> 00:26:58.900
Under section three, again,

00:26:58.900 --> 00:27:03.630
a section that's often overlooked,

00:27:03.630 --> 00:27:07.483
but is very important because it links

00:27:07.483 --> 00:27:10.671
the inner hidden essence of value

00:27:10.671 --> 00:27:13.870
with the observable forms of appearance,

00:27:13.870 --> 00:27:17.290
it's actually one of my favorite sections

00:27:18.173 --> 00:27:20.899
that so much blood sweat and tears

00:27:20.899 --> 00:27:25.523
that I have come to
appreciate it's significant.

00:27:27.910 --> 00:27:32.910
So, as I mentioned, the
logic in section three

00:27:32.950 --> 00:27:37.023
is a continuation of the
logic in sections one and two.

00:27:38.540 --> 00:27:41.189
We have already seen that
Marx explicitly noted

00:27:41.189 --> 00:27:45.500
in section one that
this logical continuity

00:27:45.500 --> 00:27:47.130
between the sections.

00:27:47.130 --> 00:27:49.300
So as previewed in section one,

00:27:49.300 --> 00:27:53.140
section three derives
money as the necessary form

00:27:53.140 --> 00:27:54.777
of appearance of the substance,

00:27:54.777 --> 00:27:58.920
and magnitude of value
in sections one, two.

00:27:58.920 --> 00:28:01.400
And very briefly summarize Marx's argument

00:28:01.400 --> 00:28:05.820
in section three is as follows.

00:28:05.820 --> 00:28:09.830
The necessity of money
arises from the fact that

00:28:09.830 --> 00:28:12.331
the substance and magnitude of value

00:28:12.331 --> 00:28:15.490
are not directly observable as such,

00:28:15.490 --> 00:28:19.524
in units of labor time,
as already mentioned,

00:28:19.524 --> 00:28:23.080
so, this is what Marx meant in section one

00:28:23.080 --> 00:28:27.235
by Phantom-like objectively
means not observable.

00:28:27.235 --> 00:28:31.700
Therefore, the question
addressed in section three

00:28:31.700 --> 00:28:33.260
is the following.

00:28:33.260 --> 00:28:37.900
How do the presupposed
unobservable quantities

00:28:37.900 --> 00:28:41.470
of socially necessary labor
time contained in commodities

00:28:41.470 --> 00:28:43.049
as previously derived,

00:28:43.049 --> 00:28:47.930
how does socially necessary
labor time acquire

00:28:47.930 --> 00:28:51.200
an objective observable form of appearance

00:28:51.200 --> 00:28:55.210
so that the value of each
commodity can in fact,

00:28:55.210 --> 00:28:58.161
in practice, be compared with the value

00:28:58.161 --> 00:28:59.933
of all other commodities?

00:29:00.840 --> 00:29:02.444
That's the subject that Marx promise

00:29:02.444 --> 00:29:06.433
twice in subsequent to
return to as we have seen,

00:29:07.330 --> 00:29:10.251
and the progress of boxes investigation

00:29:10.251 --> 00:29:14.040
has indeed led back to exchange value

00:29:14.040 --> 00:29:16.800
as the necessary form of appearance

00:29:16.800 --> 00:29:19.853
of the substance and magnitude of value.

00:29:20.760 --> 00:29:25.233
Marx posed his question in section three.

00:29:27.380 --> 00:29:30.480
In the introduction to
section three, as follows,

00:29:30.480 --> 00:29:33.043
he says "not an atom of matter,

00:29:34.377 --> 00:29:39.377
"enters into the objectivity
of commodities, as values."

00:29:39.510 --> 00:29:43.012
It is in this sense, the direct opposite

00:29:43.012 --> 00:29:47.836
of course, the sentious
objectivities as physical objects.

00:29:47.836 --> 00:29:51.777
Says, "we may twist, we're
analyzing this commodity,

00:29:51.777 --> 00:29:53.740
"this representative commodity," right?

00:29:53.740 --> 00:29:54.573
And he said,

00:29:54.573 --> 00:29:58.460
"we may twist and turn
this commodity as we wish."

00:29:58.460 --> 00:29:59.837
Right?

00:29:59.837 --> 00:30:02.143
"It remains impossible to grasp,

00:30:03.228 --> 00:30:05.028
"it remains impossible to observe it

00:30:06.387 --> 00:30:08.267
"as a thing possessing value."

00:30:10.490 --> 00:30:13.466
So, then Marx's general
answer in section three

00:30:13.466 --> 00:30:18.466
to this important question of
how unobservable quantities

00:30:20.190 --> 00:30:23.199
of abstract labor obtains
an observable form

00:30:23.199 --> 00:30:26.880
of appearance is that the
quantity of abstract labor

00:30:26.880 --> 00:30:30.900
of socially necessary labor
time in any given commodity

00:30:30.900 --> 00:30:33.080
is objectively expressed

00:30:33.080 --> 00:30:36.673
in terms of the physical
quantity of another commodity,

00:30:37.720 --> 00:30:41.823
the equivalent commodity and
ultimately the money commodity

00:30:41.823 --> 00:30:43.263
and this equivalent commodity

00:30:43.263 --> 00:30:45.713
is equal to the given commodity

00:30:45.713 --> 00:30:49.666
because it contains the same quantity

00:30:49.666 --> 00:30:52.130
of socially necessary labor time.

00:30:52.130 --> 00:30:55.240
So, by equating these two commodities

00:30:55.240 --> 00:30:57.571
that have the same quantity of labor time,

00:30:57.571 --> 00:31:02.571
the magnitude, substance
and magnitude of value

00:31:02.650 --> 00:31:04.125
in the first commodity is expressed

00:31:04.125 --> 00:31:06.486
in terms of the physical quantity

00:31:06.486 --> 00:31:09.069
of the equivalent commodity

00:31:09.069 --> 00:31:12.710
that contains the same
quantity of labor time.

00:31:15.960 --> 00:31:20.960
Marx says, "we started from exchange value

00:31:22.617 --> 00:31:25.197
"or the exchange relation
of commodities in order

00:31:25.197 --> 00:31:29.103
"to track down the value that lay hidden

00:31:29.103 --> 00:31:32.427
"unobservable within it,

00:31:32.427 --> 00:31:36.170
"we must now return to this
form of appearance of value."

00:31:36.170 --> 00:31:39.772
So, the logic is from the hidden

00:31:39.772 --> 00:31:43.533
to the observable from the observable.

00:31:44.770 --> 00:31:46.700
The observable form of appearance

00:31:46.700 --> 00:31:51.700
is derived from the hidden
substance in magnitude of value.

00:31:53.300 --> 00:31:54.980
And perhaps the clearest evidence

00:31:54.980 --> 00:31:59.090
that section three presumes,

00:31:59.090 --> 00:32:02.520
that each commodity
contains a definite quantity

00:32:02.520 --> 00:32:05.910
of abstract labor, and that
these presumed quantities

00:32:05.910 --> 00:32:07.860
of abstract labor are used to provide

00:32:07.860 --> 00:32:12.730
a quantitative labor
theory of exchange value

00:32:12.730 --> 00:32:14.383
is one subsection in particular,

00:32:14.383 --> 00:32:17.320
of the simple form of value

00:32:17.320 --> 00:32:20.900
that is entitled the
quantitative determinacy

00:32:20.900 --> 00:32:23.113
of the relative form of value.

00:32:24.592 --> 00:32:29.437
Pages 144 to 146 in the Penguin Edition.

00:32:31.761 --> 00:32:35.460
A very important and often
overlooked subsection.

00:32:38.800 --> 00:32:40.880
In this subsection Marx emphasized

00:32:40.880 --> 00:32:42.940
that the abstract labor contained

00:32:42.940 --> 00:32:46.108
in commodities is presupposed,

00:32:46.108 --> 00:32:51.108
and it must be expressed not
only in qualitative terms

00:32:51.890 --> 00:32:55.270
as the same kind of
homogeneous human labor,

00:32:55.270 --> 00:32:58.570
but also must be expressed
in quantitative terms

00:32:58.570 --> 00:33:03.341
as a definite quantity
of magnitude of value

00:33:03.341 --> 00:33:06.003
contained in commodities,

00:33:07.480 --> 00:33:10.163
socially necessary labor
time is a quantity.

00:33:11.040 --> 00:33:14.260
And therefore, its
objective form of appearance

00:33:14.260 --> 00:33:16.143
must also be quantity.

00:33:18.420 --> 00:33:20.690
So, in this subsection it is clearly

00:33:20.690 --> 00:33:24.230
and explicitly assumed that any commodity

00:33:24.230 --> 00:33:27.443
says, contains a definite
quantity of human labor.

00:33:28.309 --> 00:33:30.093
That's the presupposition.

00:33:31.067 --> 00:33:34.417
"Equality of linen and coats" Marx says,

00:33:34.417 --> 00:33:37.373
"presupposes the presence of exactly

00:33:37.373 --> 00:33:40.037
"as much of the substance of value

00:33:40.037 --> 00:33:42.277
"or the same quantity of labor time

00:33:42.277 --> 00:33:44.527
"in the two commodities."

00:33:44.527 --> 00:33:47.170
This subsection also discusses

00:33:47.170 --> 00:33:51.310
how the quantitative
expression of the linen

00:33:51.310 --> 00:33:56.310
in terms of the exchange value with coats,

00:33:56.640 --> 00:34:00.360
changes the quantitative
expression of the value

00:34:00.360 --> 00:34:03.520
of the linen in terms of
the quantity of coats,

00:34:03.520 --> 00:34:07.480
this quantitative ratio
changes if the labor time

00:34:07.480 --> 00:34:10.490
and the quantity in either the linen

00:34:10.490 --> 00:34:12.900
or the coat changes.

00:34:12.900 --> 00:34:15.700
The presupposed quantities
of labor time change,

00:34:15.700 --> 00:34:18.642
Marx discusses this, for cases, right?

00:34:18.642 --> 00:34:21.430
First, the labor time required to produce

00:34:21.430 --> 00:34:24.500
the linen changes, then
the labor time required

00:34:24.500 --> 00:34:27.260
to produce the coat changes
and then both together

00:34:27.260 --> 00:34:29.450
and then in different directions.

00:34:29.450 --> 00:34:31.870
But in each case,

00:34:31.870 --> 00:34:35.380
the labor time contained
in the commodities

00:34:35.380 --> 00:34:38.893
is presupposed, and that
then determines the exchange.

00:34:39.770 --> 00:34:41.548
So, I have an extensive excerpt

00:34:41.548 --> 00:34:44.092
from this subsection in my paper.

00:34:44.092 --> 00:34:48.050
And I will come back
to this later as well.

00:34:48.050 --> 00:34:49.580
But the basic logic I think,

00:34:49.580 --> 00:34:52.094
is very clear from the
presuppose labor times

00:34:52.094 --> 00:34:56.493
in production to exchange
values in exchange.

00:34:59.280 --> 00:35:01.639
So, these conclusions with respect,

00:35:01.639 --> 00:35:06.000
this is in a subsection of
the simple form of value.

00:35:06.000 --> 00:35:11.000
But the same conclusions apply as well

00:35:11.352 --> 00:35:14.670
to the expanded form of value,

00:35:14.670 --> 00:35:16.440
the general form of value,

00:35:16.440 --> 00:35:20.770
and ultimately the money form
of value and money prices,

00:35:20.770 --> 00:35:24.050
money prices are determined
by relative quantities

00:35:24.050 --> 00:35:25.970
of labor time and production,

00:35:25.970 --> 00:35:27.451
and changes in exchange values

00:35:27.451 --> 00:35:31.710
are determined by changes
in these relative quantities

00:35:31.710 --> 00:35:33.313
of labor time required to produce

00:35:33.313 --> 00:35:36.113
a given commodity and equivalent.

00:35:38.210 --> 00:35:41.933
In subsection C, of section three,

00:35:43.010 --> 00:35:44.790
the general form of value,

00:35:44.790 --> 00:35:47.563
is defined in terms of equations,

00:35:48.480 --> 00:35:52.510
emphasize the relation of equality

00:35:52.510 --> 00:35:57.000
between the relative and
the equivalent forms.

00:35:57.000 --> 00:36:00.200
One coat is equal to 20 yards of linen,

00:36:00.200 --> 00:36:01.920
or 10 pounds of (indistinct)

00:36:01.920 --> 00:36:04.600
equal to 20 pounds of linen
or 40 pounds of cotton

00:36:04.600 --> 00:36:07.500
is equal to 20 pounds
of linen, etc, right?

00:36:07.500 --> 00:36:11.020
So all these equations presuppose

00:36:11.020 --> 00:36:14.846
that the two commodities on
either side of the equation

00:36:14.846 --> 00:36:17.464
contain the same property and contain

00:36:17.464 --> 00:36:21.920
an equal magnitude of the same commodity.

00:36:21.920 --> 00:36:23.523
That's what an equation means.

00:36:26.020 --> 00:36:29.038
And Marx emphasized again,

00:36:29.038 --> 00:36:32.718
in this section that the
general form of value

00:36:32.718 --> 00:36:34.443
is not only quality,

00:36:37.613 --> 00:36:39.960
it's not only an expression
of abstract labor

00:36:39.960 --> 00:36:41.100
as a common property,

00:36:41.100 --> 00:36:46.100
but that this expression of
value is quantitative, right?

00:36:47.214 --> 00:36:51.110
And expresses this in this general form

00:36:51.110 --> 00:36:53.750
of value when all commodities are counted.

00:36:53.750 --> 00:36:57.548
Now, finally, as
comparable, with the linen,

00:36:57.548 --> 00:37:01.863
all commodities appear not
only as qualitatively equal,

00:37:02.850 --> 00:37:03.980
as value in general,

00:37:03.980 --> 00:37:08.473
but also as values of quantitatively
comparable magnitudes.

00:37:11.480 --> 00:37:16.480
So, section D of subsection
D of section three

00:37:17.330 --> 00:37:20.250
is entitled the money form of value.

00:37:20.250 --> 00:37:21.800
And in this subsection,

00:37:21.800 --> 00:37:24.657
the price of a commodity is defined

00:37:24.657 --> 00:37:27.814
as the exchange value of a given commodity

00:37:27.814 --> 00:37:30.200
with the money commodity.

00:37:30.200 --> 00:37:32.780
And this price is determined as before,

00:37:32.780 --> 00:37:35.510
by the relative quantities of labor time,

00:37:35.510 --> 00:37:39.760
presupposed to be contained
in the given commodity,

00:37:39.760 --> 00:37:41.083
and the money commodity.

00:37:42.530 --> 00:37:45.650
So the price of a
commodity in terms of gold,

00:37:45.650 --> 00:37:48.130
is the quantity of gold, right?

00:37:48.130 --> 00:37:50.500
A physical quantity, a quantity of gold,

00:37:50.500 --> 00:37:53.070
that contains the same
amount of labor time

00:37:53.070 --> 00:37:57.200
as the given commodity whose
price is being determined.

00:37:57.200 --> 00:37:59.510
And here there's another set of equations

00:38:01.540 --> 00:38:02.650
similar to the equations

00:38:02.650 --> 00:38:04.750
for the general form of value,

00:38:04.750 --> 00:38:07.820
except that the equivalent
commodity is gold,

00:38:07.820 --> 00:38:09.140
rather than linen,

00:38:09.140 --> 00:38:11.940
so there's a gold price for each

00:38:11.940 --> 00:38:13.310
and every other commodity,

00:38:13.310 --> 00:38:16.520
which of course presumes
that gold and each

00:38:16.520 --> 00:38:18.550
of the other commodities,

00:38:18.550 --> 00:38:22.363
contain the same quantity
socially necessary.

00:38:24.920 --> 00:38:28.070
Then, in the first edition of Volume I,

00:38:29.210 --> 00:38:30.630
unfortunately, not in late,

00:38:30.630 --> 00:38:33.680
Marx concluded his derivation of money

00:38:33.680 --> 00:38:37.300
and prices as the necessary
form of appearance of value

00:38:37.300 --> 00:38:39.423
by emphasizing once again,

00:38:41.120 --> 00:38:44.770
the overall logical structure here,

00:38:44.770 --> 00:38:47.510
and that the form of value what he says,

00:38:47.510 --> 00:38:52.170
arises out of, or is derived from

00:38:52.170 --> 00:38:55.940
the substance and the magnitude of value.

00:38:55.940 --> 00:38:59.467
Marx says, "what was decisively important

00:38:59.467 --> 00:39:03.387
"was to discover the
inner necessary connection

00:39:03.387 --> 00:39:07.997
"between value form, value substance

00:39:07.997 --> 00:39:10.656
"and value amount," right?

00:39:10.656 --> 00:39:13.437
"And express conceptually," he says,

00:39:13.437 --> 00:39:17.387
"to prove that the value form arises

00:39:17.387 --> 00:39:19.617
"out of the value concept."

00:39:21.320 --> 00:39:22.153
Right?

00:39:22.153 --> 00:39:26.930
So I think these many
passages in chapter one,

00:39:28.430 --> 00:39:31.400
provide very strong,
unambiguous textual evidence

00:39:31.400 --> 00:39:32.913
to support the interpretation.

00:39:33.770 --> 00:39:36.170
Interpretation that chapter one

00:39:36.170 --> 00:39:38.624
presupposes that individual commodities

00:39:38.624 --> 00:39:41.070
contain definite quantities of socially

00:39:41.070 --> 00:39:45.040
necessary labor time as derived
in sections one and two.

00:39:45.040 --> 00:39:48.040
And then section three
derives money prices

00:39:48.040 --> 00:39:50.310
as the necessary form of appearance

00:39:50.310 --> 00:39:53.470
of these quantities of presupposed

00:39:53.470 --> 00:39:55.580
quantities of abstract labor.

00:39:55.580 --> 00:39:58.375
That's the inner necessary connection

00:39:58.375 --> 00:40:02.400
between sections one and
two and three of chapter one

00:40:02.400 --> 00:40:04.252
and that's the overall logical structure

00:40:04.252 --> 00:40:09.252
of Marx's labor theory of value and money

00:40:09.300 --> 00:40:12.567
and abstract prices in chapter one.

00:40:15.300 --> 00:40:17.026
Okay, how am I doing in time?

