00:47:03.670 --> 00:47:08.420
So, let me just review a
few of the key quotations.

00:47:08.420 --> 00:47:11.950
So we'll have my interpretation,

00:47:11.950 --> 00:47:16.090
is we saw Marx define
socially necessary labor time,

00:47:16.090 --> 00:47:19.540
as the labor time required to produce

00:47:19.540 --> 00:47:23.460
any use value, under the
conditions of production,

00:47:23.460 --> 00:47:25.390
normal for a given society

00:47:25.390 --> 00:47:28.060
and with the average degree
of skill and intensity

00:47:28.060 --> 00:47:30.890
of labor prevalent in that society.

00:47:30.890 --> 00:47:33.130
The next paragraph Marx summarize,

00:47:33.130 --> 00:47:37.600
what exclusively determines the magnitude

00:47:37.600 --> 00:47:38.930
and value of any article,

00:47:38.930 --> 00:47:40.630
is therefore the labor time

00:47:40.630 --> 00:47:44.140
socially necessary for
its production, right?

00:47:44.140 --> 00:47:48.800
So, nothing is said here
about exchange and demand,

00:47:48.800 --> 00:47:52.515
what exclusively determines
socially necessary labor time

00:47:52.515 --> 00:47:56.260
is the average labor time in production.

00:47:56.260 --> 00:47:58.537
And coming back to the issue

00:47:58.537 --> 00:48:01.110
of the inverse relationship between

00:48:01.110 --> 00:48:04.170
the magnitude of value and
the productivity of labor,

00:48:04.170 --> 00:48:06.190
toward the end of section one,

00:48:06.190 --> 00:48:08.460
Marx discussed this relationship

00:48:08.460 --> 00:48:10.937
and he said "the value of a commodity

00:48:10.937 --> 00:48:13.097
"will therefore remain constant,

00:48:13.097 --> 00:48:16.277
"if the value would remain constant,

00:48:16.277 --> 00:48:19.557
"if the labor time
required for its production

00:48:19.557 --> 00:48:22.050
"also remain constant," right?

00:48:22.050 --> 00:48:23.390
So, even though supply and demand

00:48:23.390 --> 00:48:25.955
may be changing around
all over the place, right?

00:48:25.955 --> 00:48:30.460
If the quantity required for
its production is constant,

00:48:30.460 --> 00:48:33.430
the value is constant, right?

00:48:33.430 --> 00:48:36.573
But the ladder changes,
the productivity changes,

00:48:37.615 --> 00:48:40.558
well, value changes with every variation

00:48:40.558 --> 00:48:42.130
in the productivity of labor.

00:48:42.130 --> 00:48:43.257
And he says,

00:48:43.257 --> 00:48:45.267
"the productivity of labor is determined

00:48:45.267 --> 00:48:47.527
"by various circumstances,

00:48:47.527 --> 00:48:50.317
"the average amount of
skill of the workman,

00:48:50.317 --> 00:48:52.207
"the state of science, the degree

00:48:52.207 --> 00:48:53.767
"of its practical application,

00:48:53.767 --> 00:48:55.767
"the social organization of production,

00:48:55.767 --> 00:48:59.686
"the extent and capabilities
of the means of production,

00:48:59.686 --> 00:49:02.520
"by physical conditions, etc."

00:49:02.520 --> 00:49:03.353
Right?

00:49:03.353 --> 00:49:06.620
So, these are
characteristics of production

00:49:06.620 --> 00:49:10.143
that affect socially necessary labor time.

00:49:11.150 --> 00:49:14.270
In general, the greater
the productivity of labor,

00:49:14.270 --> 00:49:15.812
the less is the labor time required

00:49:15.812 --> 00:49:17.814
to produce that article

00:49:17.814 --> 00:49:22.260
and the less therefore is
the value and vice versa.

00:49:22.260 --> 00:49:24.634
He says, "the value of
a commodity therefore,

00:49:24.634 --> 00:49:27.377
"varies inversely with the

00:49:27.377 --> 00:49:30.420
"productivity of the
labor in (indistinct)."

00:49:41.590 --> 00:49:45.114
Well, I also wanted to
quote from that subsection

00:49:45.114 --> 00:49:50.114
on the quantitative determination
of the form of value,

00:49:52.000 --> 00:49:57.000
but I must have misplaced that page.

00:49:59.410 --> 00:50:02.000
But it's again, I hope you
will look at it, right?

00:50:02.000 --> 00:50:04.463
Page 144, five and six.

00:50:05.750 --> 00:50:10.750
This is the labor times are presupposed,

00:50:10.940 --> 00:50:15.133
and that then determines
the relative form.

00:50:26.980 --> 00:50:28.770
- Do you feel like there's enough evidence

00:50:28.770 --> 00:50:32.130
on the table to rest
your case temporarily?

00:50:32.130 --> 00:50:32.963
- Oh, yes, yes, yes.

00:50:32.963 --> 00:50:37.842
I also wanted to discuss the value form.

00:50:46.480 --> 00:50:48.183
Sorry, I lost my page there.

00:50:54.920 --> 00:50:55.753
Okay.

00:50:55.753 --> 00:51:00.753
So the value form interpretation,
in contrast, right?

00:51:00.830 --> 00:51:05.830
Argues that if supply
is greater than demand

00:51:06.080 --> 00:51:08.970
in an industry, then the
price of the commodity

00:51:08.970 --> 00:51:12.892
will fall and the socially
necessary labor time

00:51:12.892 --> 00:51:16.023
will also be reduced, right?

00:51:16.023 --> 00:51:21.023
Because of the insufficient social demand,

00:51:21.530 --> 00:51:25.520
even though even if the average labor time

00:51:25.520 --> 00:51:27.253
in production remains the same.

00:51:28.270 --> 00:51:33.127
However, and this is the point
we touched on a minute ago,

00:51:33.127 --> 00:51:37.113
I argue that Marx's theory in capital,

00:51:38.030 --> 00:51:42.647
generally assumes that supply
is equal to demand, right?

00:51:42.647 --> 00:51:46.147
And so assumes a way that case

00:51:46.147 --> 00:51:51.147
except for a few asides here and there.

00:51:51.450 --> 00:51:54.540
In other words, Marx's
theory of value and capital

00:51:54.540 --> 00:51:58.173
is not about the daily
fluctuation of market prices,

00:51:58.173 --> 00:52:02.503
but is instead about the center of gravity

00:52:02.503 --> 00:52:05.285
of those market prices.

00:52:05.285 --> 00:52:06.830
The equilibrium prices,

00:52:06.830 --> 00:52:08.200
the long run average

00:52:08.200 --> 00:52:09.150
which are the centers

00:52:09.150 --> 00:52:12.063
of gravity around which market prices.

00:52:13.340 --> 00:52:15.150
The main question as I said,

00:52:15.150 --> 00:52:17.591
in Marx's theory is to
explain surplus value

00:52:17.591 --> 00:52:22.240
and the inner law of surplus
value with surplus labor,

00:52:22.240 --> 00:52:26.430
and to consider fluctuations
due to supply and demand.

00:52:26.430 --> 00:52:28.469
Marx argued at the end of chapter five

00:52:28.469 --> 00:52:31.712
would be a distraction from discovering

00:52:31.712 --> 00:52:36.260
and pinpointing the inner
law of surplus value.

00:52:36.260 --> 00:52:39.090
So, I present more textual
evidence in my paper

00:52:39.090 --> 00:52:42.180
to support this supplies equal to demand,

00:52:42.180 --> 00:52:45.314
equilibrium interpretation
of Marx's theory of value,

00:52:45.314 --> 00:52:48.380
and would be happy to discuss these later,

00:52:48.380 --> 00:52:52.630
and I think the evidence is conclusive

00:52:52.630 --> 00:52:55.799
that Marx is trying to explain

00:52:55.799 --> 00:52:58.090
the inner laws of capitalism

00:52:58.090 --> 00:53:01.823
and not the daily
fluctuations of market prices.

00:53:03.680 --> 00:53:08.680
And since equilibrium prices
assume supply is equal demand,

00:53:10.000 --> 00:53:12.750
then in that case, supply and demand

00:53:12.750 --> 00:53:15.050
have no effect on the determination

00:53:15.050 --> 00:53:17.670
of equilibrium prices.

00:53:17.670 --> 00:53:19.470
Cancel each other out.

00:53:19.470 --> 00:53:20.901
Equilibrium prices are determined

00:53:20.901 --> 00:53:23.508
in that case solely by the fundamental

00:53:23.508 --> 00:53:27.473
cause of average labor
times in production.

00:53:28.370 --> 00:53:32.150
Supply and demand,
determine market prices,

00:53:32.150 --> 00:53:34.610
not equilibrium prices.

00:53:34.610 --> 00:53:38.054
Furthermore, if supply
is not equal to demand

00:53:38.054 --> 00:53:40.638
in an industry, which is usually the case,

00:53:40.638 --> 00:53:43.090
the market price of the commodity

00:53:43.090 --> 00:53:46.550
will be different from
the equilibrium price,

00:53:46.550 --> 00:53:47.770
but the equilibrium price

00:53:47.770 --> 00:53:51.060
will nonetheless remain the same, right?

00:53:51.060 --> 00:53:52.570
And will still be determined

00:53:52.570 --> 00:53:54.796
by the average labor time in production

00:53:54.796 --> 00:53:57.370
even though supply and
demand are not equal

00:53:57.370 --> 00:53:58.968
and market prices are fluctuating around

00:53:58.968 --> 00:54:01.270
the equilibrium price.

00:54:01.270 --> 00:54:02.927
Equilibrium price will change only

00:54:02.927 --> 00:54:07.873
if the average labor time
in production changes.

00:54:08.870 --> 00:54:10.033
One more page.

00:54:11.115 --> 00:54:14.240
Michael wrote in his book,

00:54:14.240 --> 00:54:15.953
that socially necessary labor time

00:54:15.953 --> 00:54:19.690
is determined by three reductions.

00:54:19.690 --> 00:54:22.340
And this was actually very helpful to me.

00:54:22.340 --> 00:54:26.090
One, reduction of unequal
productivities of labor

00:54:26.090 --> 00:54:29.970
to average productivity
within each industry.

00:54:29.970 --> 00:54:31.605
Two, the reduction of skilled labor

00:54:31.605 --> 00:54:35.190
to unskilled labor in all industries

00:54:35.190 --> 00:54:39.497
and three, the reduction
of supply to demand, right?

00:54:41.360 --> 00:54:44.565
However, if it is assumed that supply is

00:54:44.565 --> 00:54:49.565
equal to demand, then Michael's
reduction number three

00:54:49.990 --> 00:54:54.990
of supply to demand is
nullified, plays no role, right?

00:54:55.370 --> 00:54:58.612
And in this case, socially
necessary labor time

00:54:58.612 --> 00:55:02.563
is determined by the first two reductions,

00:55:02.563 --> 00:55:05.400
unequal productivities
to average productivity,

00:55:05.400 --> 00:55:08.600
skilled labor to unskilled labor, right?

00:55:08.600 --> 00:55:10.540
And these two remaining reductions

00:55:10.540 --> 00:55:13.340
are characteristics of production

00:55:13.340 --> 00:55:18.340
and determinants of
labor time in production.

00:55:18.600 --> 00:55:22.900
Thus, if it is assumed that
supply is equal to demand

00:55:22.900 --> 00:55:25.750
as Marx generally assumed in capital,

00:55:25.750 --> 00:55:28.360
then demand and exchange have no effect

00:55:28.360 --> 00:55:32.470
on the determination of
socially necessary labor time,

00:55:32.470 --> 00:55:35.780
even with Michael's interpretation

00:55:35.780 --> 00:55:37.930
of socially necessary labor time

00:55:37.930 --> 00:55:41.820
because the supply is
assumed to be equal to demand

00:55:41.820 --> 00:55:46.820
and that reduction is not necessary.

00:55:48.360 --> 00:55:52.820
So, I hope Michael will agree that under

00:55:52.820 --> 00:55:56.280
the assumption of supply is
equal to demand at least,

00:55:56.280 --> 00:56:00.360
socially necessary labor
time depends only on the

00:56:00.360 --> 00:56:02.433
average labor time in production.

00:56:03.650 --> 00:56:08.370
And if supply is not equal to demand,

00:56:08.370 --> 00:56:12.300
then we have I think, two
different interpretations

00:56:12.300 --> 00:56:13.872
of socially necessary labor time,

00:56:13.872 --> 00:56:17.773
which may in fact, be compatible,

00:56:19.010 --> 00:56:22.430
be mutually compatible and
not mutually exclusive,

00:56:22.430 --> 00:56:25.473
they apply to different cases, right?

00:56:28.320 --> 00:56:31.961
One an equilibrium concept of
socially necessary labor time

00:56:31.961 --> 00:56:35.860
determined by average
labor time in production

00:56:35.860 --> 00:56:37.480
and independent of demand,

00:56:37.480 --> 00:56:40.150
because you're assuming that away, right?

00:56:40.150 --> 00:56:42.950
And secondly, a disequilibrium concept

00:56:42.950 --> 00:56:44.183
of socially necessary.

00:56:45.070 --> 00:56:49.720
Labor time received
through exchange, right?

00:56:49.720 --> 00:56:54.010
Which also depends on important demand.

00:56:54.010 --> 00:56:56.270
And it has occurred to me in recent weeks

00:56:56.270 --> 00:56:57.607
while working on this paper

00:56:57.607 --> 00:57:02.607
that maybe we agree on this point

00:57:02.850 --> 00:57:05.360
more than I thought, right?

00:57:05.360 --> 00:57:09.150
In the case of supply is equal demand,

00:57:09.150 --> 00:57:12.303
I hope we can agree that
socially necessary labor time

00:57:12.303 --> 00:57:14.890
is the average labor time in production,

00:57:14.890 --> 00:57:19.080
which determines the
equilibrium price, right?

00:57:19.080 --> 00:57:22.960
If so, then the disagreement
would really boil down to

00:57:24.640 --> 00:57:27.856
in the case of supply,
not equal to demand.

00:57:27.856 --> 00:57:31.210
What's the definition of
socially necessary labor time

00:57:31.210 --> 00:57:36.210
and is the concept operative at all?

00:57:40.470 --> 00:57:45.470
It seems to me that maybe
these two definitions,

00:57:46.710 --> 00:57:49.023
I don't think Marx ever talked about this,

00:57:50.110 --> 00:57:53.290
explicitly in terms of socially
necessary labor time anyway,

00:57:53.290 --> 00:57:58.290
but I wanna search some more
and think some more about this.

00:58:00.130 --> 00:58:04.090
That the equilibrium and
the disequilibrium concepts

00:58:04.090 --> 00:58:09.090
of socially necessary labor
time may be mutually compatible.

00:58:13.377 --> 00:58:18.377
And I think this would be
a significant agreement.

00:58:19.180 --> 00:58:20.840
And, as I said, Michael's discussion

00:58:20.840 --> 00:58:23.133
of the three reductions,

00:58:24.580 --> 00:58:26.815
one of which is the reduction
of supply and demand,

00:58:26.815 --> 00:58:30.494
I think, helped me realize
at least this possibility

00:58:30.494 --> 00:58:33.247
of more of agreement.

00:58:35.340 --> 00:58:37.024
If supply is equal to demand

00:58:37.024 --> 00:58:39.600
then there are only two reductions,

00:58:39.600 --> 00:58:44.310
both of which have to do with
labor time of reductions.

00:58:44.310 --> 00:58:45.920
So I'll stop here for now

00:58:45.920 --> 00:58:49.130
and give Michael a chance to respond

00:58:49.130 --> 00:58:51.763
and see what other people have to say.

00:58:53.020 --> 00:58:55.530
Thank you very much for your attention.

00:58:55.530 --> 00:58:58.290
And I look forward to the discussion.

00:58:58.290 --> 00:58:59.330
- Thank you, Fred.

00:58:59.330 --> 00:59:01.900
Let's just take a minute
to thank Fred for his talk

00:59:01.900 --> 00:59:05.270
before we get into the
beautiful family fight.

00:59:05.270 --> 00:59:06.956
There's no better fights
and family fights,

00:59:06.956 --> 00:59:09.980
and especially when
they're about scripture.

00:59:09.980 --> 00:59:12.820
It turns out all sorts
of hermeneutic questions

00:59:12.820 --> 00:59:14.780
are on the table now too, so I think--

00:59:14.780 --> 00:59:16.319
- What sort of what questions?

00:59:16.319 --> 00:59:17.437
- [Paul] Hermeneutic questions.

00:59:17.437 --> 00:59:19.100
- Okay, yeah.

00:59:19.100 --> 00:59:20.793
- But thank you very much
for your presentation.

00:59:20.793 --> 00:59:22.700
I think Michael you'd like to respond

00:59:22.700 --> 00:59:25.450
and you could take as much
time as you want to do that.

00:59:28.160 --> 00:59:31.090
- This is dangerous
when you allow me these,

00:59:31.090 --> 00:59:34.930
to take as much time as I'd want.

00:59:34.930 --> 00:59:38.633
I try to make it briefly.

00:59:39.960 --> 00:59:42.020
But first is the connection okay.

00:59:42.020 --> 00:59:43.710
Can you understand me?

00:59:43.710 --> 00:59:44.690
Okay.

00:59:44.690 --> 00:59:47.685
So, first, I have to thank Fred

00:59:47.685 --> 00:59:52.230
that he changed his original idea

00:59:52.230 --> 00:59:57.230
of a paper and make such big efforts

00:59:59.280 --> 01:00:04.280
to discuss my approach to value theory,

01:00:05.030 --> 01:00:09.500
I think this is a very big appreciation

01:00:09.500 --> 01:00:14.500
of my work and I'm very thankful for that,

01:00:15.390 --> 01:00:20.390
that you have main
critical points is not bad

01:00:20.850 --> 01:00:22.430
because I think in this point,

01:00:22.430 --> 01:00:24.870
we will agree that critique

01:00:24.870 --> 01:00:28.030
is the method to get progress,

01:00:28.030 --> 01:00:30.610
nothing is final nothing is perfect.

01:00:30.610 --> 01:00:33.601
So, we need critique and I appreciate

01:00:33.601 --> 01:00:36.580
your critique on my points

01:00:36.580 --> 01:00:40.800
and already in this short exchange,

01:00:40.800 --> 01:00:43.540
we had the last days,

01:00:43.540 --> 01:00:47.570
I learned a lot and maybe we will not come

01:00:47.570 --> 01:00:51.200
to an agreement, but at least also for me,

01:00:51.200 --> 01:00:54.640
it became clearer in which points

01:00:54.640 --> 01:00:58.310
I have to argue more precise,

01:00:58.310 --> 01:01:03.310
more exact, and this I learned by you.

01:01:04.040 --> 01:01:08.650
So, I want to focus on the main point

01:01:10.870 --> 01:01:15.570
we disagree is the magnitude of value

01:01:15.570 --> 01:01:19.320
already determined by production

01:01:19.320 --> 01:01:22.410
before the exchange process

01:01:22.410 --> 01:01:27.410
or is the magnitude of
value not determined

01:01:28.270 --> 01:01:30.950
by production alone and in (indistinct)

01:01:30.950 --> 01:01:35.320
also not before the exchange process?

01:01:35.320 --> 01:01:40.040
Already one person asked
before what is on stake,

01:01:40.040 --> 01:01:41.540
what are the consequences

01:01:41.540 --> 01:01:44.900
of these different interpretations?

01:01:44.900 --> 01:01:49.900
And I think he not Fred the person

01:01:50.940 --> 01:01:55.940
who asked came to the conclusion, okay,

01:01:56.470 --> 01:01:59.860
it strengthens revolutionary
when everything

01:01:59.860 --> 01:02:04.440
is rested in production,

01:02:04.440 --> 01:02:07.010
I think this is rather independent

01:02:07.010 --> 01:02:11.417
from any revolutionary activities

01:02:12.530 --> 01:02:16.170
or from insights which lead to revolution,

01:02:16.170 --> 01:02:18.920
but there is something on stake,

01:02:18.920 --> 01:02:23.920
when everything already is
determined by production,

01:02:24.920 --> 01:02:28.400
then at least in principle,

01:02:28.400 --> 01:02:31.140
we can know everything,

01:02:31.140 --> 01:02:35.010
we can know all the magnitude of value,

01:02:35.010 --> 01:02:39.660
we can treat value not as an outcome

01:02:40.850 --> 01:02:44.890
which emerges like Marx says in the back

01:02:44.890 --> 01:02:49.890
of the producers, but we can
treat them as known amounts.

01:02:53.090 --> 01:02:56.900
And exactly this, in Soviet Union

01:02:56.900 --> 01:03:01.100
in the so-called real
socialism planning economy

01:03:01.100 --> 01:03:05.670
was attempted, there was the theory of,

01:03:05.670 --> 01:03:08.110
they called it the conscious

01:03:08.110 --> 01:03:11.840
application of the law of value.

01:03:11.840 --> 01:03:16.840
And this conscious application
exactly presupposed

01:03:17.160 --> 01:03:21.670
that we know already by production

01:03:21.670 --> 01:03:24.450
without an process of exchange,

01:03:24.450 --> 01:03:27.520
what are the magnitudes of value

01:03:27.520 --> 01:03:32.070
and this planning process
was an enormous failure.

01:03:32.070 --> 01:03:35.870
So for this, the wrong theory they had.

01:03:35.870 --> 01:03:38.380
And of course, not only this was wrong,

01:03:38.380 --> 01:03:40.643
I don't want to reduce

01:03:41.560 --> 01:03:44.000
all the failure of Soviet Union

01:03:44.000 --> 01:03:45.330
to this single point.

01:03:45.330 --> 01:03:49.670
But this point, also contributed to this.

01:03:49.670 --> 01:03:53.100
It had some revival some years ago,

01:03:53.100 --> 01:03:58.100
in the discussions of a
socialism of the 21st century,

01:04:00.050 --> 01:04:04.250
when, for example, authors
like Paul (indistinct)

01:04:04.250 --> 01:04:07.570
argued by the use of computer

01:04:07.570 --> 01:04:12.330
now we have the technology to make use

01:04:12.330 --> 01:04:15.813
of all these knowledge and we can use

01:04:17.122 --> 01:04:20.440
this knowledge we have
from the production process

01:04:20.440 --> 01:04:24.940
of commodity production
for a socialist system,

01:04:24.940 --> 01:04:27.880
I think this is totally wrong.

01:04:27.880 --> 01:04:32.670
And when you try to do a socialist system,

01:04:32.670 --> 01:04:37.670
according to such assumptions,
it will be disastrous.

01:04:38.000 --> 01:04:41.860
So indeed, there is something on stage.

01:04:41.860 --> 01:04:46.790
It's not only a question of details,

01:04:46.790 --> 01:04:51.790
or pure science, it has
political consequences.

01:04:54.415 --> 01:04:57.120
- Michael, can I ask
you just on this point?

01:04:57.120 --> 01:04:58.710
- [Michael] Yeah.

01:04:58.710 --> 01:05:00.750
It seemed to me that Fred was saying that

01:05:00.750 --> 01:05:03.850
as a manifestation of value,

01:05:03.850 --> 01:05:07.890
exchange value allows you to know

01:05:07.890 --> 01:05:10.460
what's determined in production.

01:05:10.460 --> 01:05:12.730
Do you mean that when you say

01:05:12.730 --> 01:05:15.500
you can't treat them as known amounts,

01:05:15.500 --> 01:05:17.490
the magnitudes of value,

01:05:17.490 --> 01:05:20.660
do you mean that they're actually

01:05:20.660 --> 01:05:23.350
being determined in exchange,

01:05:23.350 --> 01:05:25.860
or they're just coming
to be known in exchange.

01:05:25.860 --> 01:05:26.930
I think there's a difference

01:05:26.930 --> 01:05:29.360
between being determined and being known.

01:05:29.360 --> 01:05:31.460
- Exactly it is a difference

01:05:31.460 --> 01:05:36.460
and they not come to be
known before exchange,

01:05:39.924 --> 01:05:42.530
they are not determined,

01:05:42.530 --> 01:05:46.150
they are as Fred quoted me correctly,

01:05:46.150 --> 01:05:49.490
they are determined by production

01:05:49.490 --> 01:05:53.330
and circulation or
production and exchange,

01:05:53.330 --> 01:05:56.810
production alone is not enough

01:05:56.810 --> 01:06:00.210
to determine magnitude of value.

01:06:00.210 --> 01:06:02.860
And it is not a question of knowledge.

01:06:02.860 --> 01:06:06.330
I will explain this at once,

01:06:06.330 --> 01:06:09.450
let me just do before one remark

01:06:09.450 --> 01:06:11.763
about different levels of discussion.

01:06:12.690 --> 01:06:16.600
We can discuss this question,

01:06:16.600 --> 01:06:18.380
where magnitude of value

01:06:18.380 --> 01:06:23.313
is determined on a hermeneutical level,

01:06:24.520 --> 01:06:26.790
interpreting Marx texts,

01:06:26.790 --> 01:06:29.260
what is the opinion of Marx,

01:06:29.260 --> 01:06:34.260
but also we have to
discuss what is the reality

01:06:35.220 --> 01:06:40.220
of commodity production
and commodity exchange

01:06:40.360 --> 01:06:44.440
and in so far the hermeneutical level

01:06:44.440 --> 01:06:47.610
is only a helper when we assume

01:06:47.610 --> 01:06:51.550
that Marx did a very good analysis.

01:06:51.550 --> 01:06:54.650
So, we must be clear about his analysis,

01:06:54.650 --> 01:06:58.820
but the point which is decisive

01:06:58.820 --> 01:07:02.680
is our analysis then of reality

01:07:02.680 --> 01:07:05.960
and not the analysis of texts of Marx.

01:07:05.960 --> 01:07:09.900
So, I think they have to
distinguish these two levels,

01:07:09.900 --> 01:07:14.747
I will come briefly to
the level of reality

01:07:15.824 --> 01:07:18.863
I take this as an abbreviation,

01:07:19.710 --> 01:07:23.540
why do I think that magnitude of value

01:07:23.540 --> 01:07:27.653
is not determined only by production,

01:07:31.830 --> 01:07:36.590
just let us restrict to
these two determinations,

01:07:36.590 --> 01:07:41.590
we agree, socially necessary labor time

01:07:42.430 --> 01:07:47.263
and the reduction of complex
labor to simple labor.

01:07:48.120 --> 01:07:53.120
Socially necessary labor
time is a kind of average,

01:07:54.090 --> 01:07:55.890
there are different kind

01:07:55.890 --> 01:07:58.930
of production processes as Marx discusses

01:07:58.930 --> 01:08:00.917
this in chapter one,

01:08:02.950 --> 01:08:07.143
the one producer is quicker
the other is slower,

01:08:08.160 --> 01:08:13.160
but what contributes to this average

01:08:13.370 --> 01:08:17.890
is not just what every producer is doing,

01:08:17.890 --> 01:08:22.070
it contributes only that products,

01:08:22.070 --> 01:08:25.020
which are brought to the market,

01:08:25.020 --> 01:08:29.180
when there is super modern factory

01:08:29.180 --> 01:08:32.020
with an extremely high productivity

01:08:33.530 --> 01:08:37.240
but the products are
hindered to enter the market

01:08:38.294 --> 01:08:40.880
or the owner of the factory decides

01:08:40.880 --> 01:08:45.470
to sell these products abroad,

01:08:45.470 --> 01:08:48.770
because he can receive
higher prices or whatever,

01:08:48.770 --> 01:08:53.770
then this factory or the
labor in this factory

01:08:55.520 --> 01:08:59.070
will not contribute to this

01:08:59.070 --> 01:09:02.130
average socially necessary labor time.

01:09:02.130 --> 01:09:06.370
So by looking only on the production side,

01:09:06.370 --> 01:09:09.510
we don't know really what contributes

01:09:09.510 --> 01:09:13.770
to this average,

01:09:13.770 --> 01:09:16.750
and even more clear,

01:09:16.750 --> 01:09:21.750
it becomes when we look to the
reduction of complex labor,

01:09:25.610 --> 01:09:28.960
to simple labor, it is
not only one reduction,

01:09:28.960 --> 01:09:33.863
you have a lot of different
kinds of complex labor,

01:09:34.770 --> 01:09:38.910
all of them must be
reduced to simple labor,

01:09:38.910 --> 01:09:43.910
let us say, one hour
of labor of a craftsman

01:09:44.670 --> 01:09:48.490
is reduced to three hours of simple labor,

01:09:48.490 --> 01:09:51.369
five hour labor of...

01:09:51.369 --> 01:09:54.810
One hour labor of an engineer

01:09:54.810 --> 01:09:58.663
is reduced to five hours of simple labor.

01:09:59.530 --> 01:10:02.393
But where does this reduction come from,

01:10:03.530 --> 01:10:06.460
easy determined by production.

01:10:06.460 --> 01:10:08.114
I don't know not even a theory

01:10:08.114 --> 01:10:13.114
who explains this reduction
by production itself,

01:10:13.200 --> 01:10:16.400
Marx speaks of a social process

01:10:16.400 --> 01:10:20.980
in the second subsection of chapter one,

01:10:20.980 --> 01:10:25.980
and in chapter seven, in the
last footnote of this chapter,

01:10:26.400 --> 01:10:31.400
he stresses that this
reduction also depends

01:10:33.630 --> 01:10:38.520
of social prejudice, for example,

01:10:38.520 --> 01:10:42.210
that it comes out in a historical process.

01:10:42.210 --> 01:10:47.210
And we have very striking examples,

01:10:47.360 --> 01:10:52.360
that for example, the
such branches of work,

01:10:53.640 --> 01:10:57.760
where women dominate as workers,

01:10:57.760 --> 01:11:02.760
usually such branches count
less in this reduction,

01:11:03.930 --> 01:11:08.930
it is not a question of the
pure complexity of labor

01:11:09.690 --> 01:11:11.620
or of the production process,

01:11:11.620 --> 01:11:16.620
it is also the part of social evaluation

01:11:17.010 --> 01:11:20.420
and this is beyond the production process.

01:11:20.420 --> 01:11:21.573
In so far,

01:11:26.270 --> 01:11:28.760
I think it is impossible

01:11:28.760 --> 01:11:33.760
to determine the magnitude
of value only by production.

01:11:36.330 --> 01:11:38.930
I don't want to go deep in the question

01:11:38.930 --> 01:11:43.930
of demand and supply, which has, I think,

01:11:44.330 --> 01:11:48.350
nothing to do with the two
points I just mentioned.

01:11:48.350 --> 01:11:51.100
But I want to stress one point,

01:11:51.100 --> 01:11:55.427
it is true that Marx
later in "Capital" says,

01:11:55.427 --> 01:12:00.427
"I presuppose demand and supply equals"

01:12:00.550 --> 01:12:04.770
and by this he can abstract
from a lot of problems,

01:12:04.770 --> 01:12:09.770
but what is the demand and
the supply which equals.

01:12:10.530 --> 01:12:14.920
How is demand and supply determined?

01:12:14.920 --> 01:12:19.920
This we must discuss
before we can assume supply

01:12:21.240 --> 01:12:23.380
and demand equals.

01:12:23.380 --> 01:12:27.460
And I think what Marx is
doing in the third chapter

01:12:27.460 --> 01:12:32.460
he gives a second meaning to
socially necessary labor time.

01:12:34.420 --> 01:12:38.510
And this second meaning then defines

01:12:38.510 --> 01:12:42.460
what is demand and only then

01:12:42.460 --> 01:12:47.070
we can discuss or we can
presuppose demand and supply

01:12:47.070 --> 01:12:52.070
we assume that it is equal,
but, okay this is another point.

01:12:53.610 --> 01:12:58.610
Now, I want shortly to come
to the hermeneutical level,

01:13:01.030 --> 01:13:04.283
what is Marx telling us?

01:13:07.030 --> 01:13:10.500
I think indeed, we have
to look to the logic

01:13:10.500 --> 01:13:15.500
of Marx argumentation,
as Fred also stressed,

01:13:18.810 --> 01:13:21.050
I would say we have to be clear,

01:13:21.050 --> 01:13:26.050
what in the first three
chapters, Marx is doing,

01:13:26.130 --> 01:13:30.600
what is the object, what
Marx wants to research

01:13:30.600 --> 01:13:33.830
in the first three chapters of Volume I.

01:13:33.830 --> 01:13:38.389
I think it is commodity circulation.

01:13:38.389 --> 01:13:42.920
This complicated process
of commodity circulation,

01:13:42.920 --> 01:13:44.620
commodity against money,

01:13:44.620 --> 01:13:46.010
money against commodity,

01:13:46.010 --> 01:13:47.950
commodity against money and so on,

01:13:47.950 --> 01:13:52.870
this permanent intersection
is reduced by Marx

01:13:52.870 --> 01:13:57.870
in a first step of abstraction
to the exchange process,

01:13:58.570 --> 01:14:02.740
commodity owners exchange commodities,

01:14:02.740 --> 01:14:07.137
and the core of the analysis
of this exchange process

01:14:08.070 --> 01:14:11.550
is the exchange relation,

01:14:11.550 --> 01:14:16.460
which Marx analyzes in chapter one.

01:14:16.460 --> 01:14:20.010
So, when we read "Capital,"

01:14:20.010 --> 01:14:23.050
we do the journey backwards,

01:14:23.050 --> 01:14:26.870
we start with the most abstract core,

01:14:26.870 --> 01:14:30.480
the exchange relation of two commodities.

01:14:30.480 --> 01:14:34.700
In chapter two we include
the commodity owners

01:14:34.700 --> 01:14:38.640
we come to the exchange process.

01:14:38.640 --> 01:14:40.307
And in chapter three,

01:14:40.307 --> 01:14:44.408
we see the intersection and interference

01:14:44.408 --> 01:14:49.408
of all the exchange
processes in the circulation

01:14:51.510 --> 01:14:53.063
in the commodity circulation.

01:14:54.440 --> 01:14:56.629
So, exchange,

01:14:56.629 --> 01:14:57.462
(clears throat)

01:14:57.462 --> 01:15:01.150
sorry my voice is not

01:15:01.150 --> 01:15:03.460
in a good condition in the moment.

01:15:03.460 --> 01:15:08.460
So, what Marx analyzes
as exchange relation

01:15:09.950 --> 01:15:12.040
is the most abstract core

01:15:12.962 --> 01:15:17.900
of the circulation process of commodities.

01:15:17.900 --> 01:15:21.140
And this exchange relation,

01:15:21.140 --> 01:15:26.140
he dissects like an anatomist.

01:15:28.990 --> 01:15:33.990
Fred gave the nice quote where Marx says,

01:15:34.757 --> 01:15:39.140
"we have to consider
value independently from,"

01:15:41.510 --> 01:15:44.333
I think it's a form of
appearance of value form.

01:15:45.530 --> 01:15:47.920
I stress that Marx wrote,

01:15:47.920 --> 01:15:50.470
we have to consider,

01:15:50.470 --> 01:15:54.380
he doesn't say value exists

01:15:54.380 --> 01:15:57.100
independently from value form.

01:15:57.100 --> 01:15:59.310
He says, we have to consider it.

01:15:59.310 --> 01:16:02.840
And so I think Marx behaves

01:16:02.840 --> 01:16:07.840
like an anatomist who wants
to analyze the human body.

01:16:08.830 --> 01:16:11.990
And he has to start with certain organs.

01:16:11.990 --> 01:16:14.690
So maybe he starts with the heart,

01:16:14.690 --> 01:16:16.103
which is pumping blood,

01:16:16.940 --> 01:16:18.690
and then comes to the lung,

01:16:18.690 --> 01:16:23.667
which is filling the blood with oxygen.

01:16:24.920 --> 01:16:29.140
But this sequence of explanation

01:16:29.140 --> 01:16:34.140
is not identical with
a sequence of existence

01:16:34.240 --> 01:16:37.170
that first the heart exists.

01:16:37.170 --> 01:16:41.170
And then after a while, the lung exists.

01:16:41.170 --> 01:16:45.610
And then after a while,
something else exists.

01:16:45.610 --> 01:16:49.823
So what I think is a mistake of Fred,

01:16:52.500 --> 01:16:57.500
is to identify the sequence of explanation

01:16:59.260 --> 01:17:04.260
we find in Marx, with a
sequence of existence,

01:17:06.650 --> 01:17:08.830
a sequence in time.

01:17:08.830 --> 01:17:12.540
And also, I think it's a mistake

01:17:12.540 --> 01:17:16.403
to see in the sequence of time,

01:17:18.875 --> 01:17:20.117
causal relationship, a causality from

01:17:24.320 --> 01:17:29.320
what he thinks is fast to that
what he thinks comes later.

01:17:30.691 --> 01:17:33.290
At the beginning of his paper,

01:17:33.290 --> 01:17:37.250
he gives this nice quote
from the first edition

01:17:37.250 --> 01:17:41.060
that Marx said I wanted to prove

01:17:41.060 --> 01:17:46.060
that value form arises
from the value concept.

01:17:46.310 --> 01:17:49.440
This is a very Hegelian wording

01:17:49.440 --> 01:17:51.054
and Marx himself said sometimes,

01:17:51.054 --> 01:17:56.054
he is pocketing with Hegelian terminology,

01:17:58.930 --> 01:18:03.440
he also becomes clear that
this terminology arises

01:18:03.440 --> 01:18:08.440
from is not a temporal sequence.

01:18:09.140 --> 01:18:12.340
When you read Hegel's signs of logic,

01:18:12.340 --> 01:18:15.330
you have the essence and the necessity

01:18:15.330 --> 01:18:19.260
that the essence needs
a form of appearance.

01:18:19.260 --> 01:18:23.590
But of course, Hegel nowhere argues

01:18:23.590 --> 01:18:28.530
that essence exists first in time,

01:18:28.530 --> 01:18:30.850
and then after a while

01:18:30.850 --> 01:18:33.920
it receives a form of appearance,

01:18:33.920 --> 01:18:37.850
and I think the same is true for value

01:18:37.850 --> 01:18:41.700
and value form as form of appearance,

01:18:41.700 --> 01:18:45.640
they can only exist simultaneously.

01:18:45.640 --> 01:18:49.230
So, what Marx is doing
in the first chapter,

01:18:49.230 --> 01:18:54.230
he is dissecting the exchange relation,

01:18:54.590 --> 01:18:56.780
he talks about value,

01:18:56.780 --> 01:19:00.790
but value inside the exchange relation,

01:19:00.790 --> 01:19:04.333
not outside the exchange relation.

01:19:05.900 --> 01:19:10.640
So, this is one important difference

01:19:10.640 --> 01:19:14.950
in the hermeneutics,
in the interpretation.

01:19:14.950 --> 01:19:19.950
I will just briefly
mention another difference.

01:19:21.930 --> 01:19:26.930
Now, Fred didn't mention
it in his lecture,

01:19:27.970 --> 01:19:30.510
but he mentioned it in the paper,

01:19:30.510 --> 01:19:33.834
he sent me and I suppose
also it was distributed

01:19:33.834 --> 01:19:38.614
to the audience,

01:19:38.614 --> 01:19:41.340
where the point is,

01:19:41.340 --> 01:19:46.340
what means the common
in the phrase that labor

01:19:46.460 --> 01:19:51.143
is the common social substance of value.

01:19:52.970 --> 01:19:55.650
Fred, absolutely correctly,

01:19:55.650 --> 01:20:00.180
quotes my interpretation that there

01:20:00.180 --> 01:20:04.830
are two different ways
to interpret this common.

01:20:04.830 --> 01:20:09.830
Common can mean that
two things independently

01:20:10.540 --> 01:20:14.670
from each other process a property

01:20:14.670 --> 01:20:19.670
or it can mean that
common means they share

01:20:20.110 --> 01:20:24.080
this property, and my interpretation

01:20:24.080 --> 01:20:26.513
is the second one, they share it.

01:20:27.360 --> 01:20:30.520
And here is the point I also want to give

01:20:30.520 --> 01:20:35.520
some textual evidence for my approach,

01:20:37.230 --> 01:20:41.120
for the approach that
also Marx had in mind,

01:20:41.120 --> 01:20:44.800
this meaning of common
as a shared property

01:20:44.800 --> 01:20:49.800
inside the exchange relation.

01:20:50.570 --> 01:20:53.590
There is a difference between the wording

01:20:53.590 --> 01:20:58.130
of the first edition and
wording of the second edition,

01:20:58.130 --> 01:21:03.130
which is totally lost in the
existing English translations.

01:21:04.270 --> 01:21:07.690
When you read the first translation,

01:21:07.690 --> 01:21:12.690
other than translation of the
first edition of "Capital,"

01:21:14.730 --> 01:21:18.630
which also Fred quotes.

01:21:18.630 --> 01:21:22.267
You can read in the
translation of (indistinct),

01:21:24.002 --> 01:21:26.680
the common social substance

01:21:26.680 --> 01:21:29.770
which merely manifests itself differently

01:21:29.770 --> 01:21:33.093
in different use values, is labor,

01:21:34.130 --> 01:21:38.850
in the second edition, you can
read a very similar sentence

01:21:41.100 --> 01:21:45.890
in the translation of
Ben Fowkes as crystals

01:21:45.890 --> 01:21:47.534
of this social substance,

01:21:47.534 --> 01:21:52.483
which is common to them
all they have values.

01:21:53.840 --> 01:21:58.840
In both English sentences,
we have the word common,

01:21:59.030 --> 01:22:03.320
but in German Marx changed the words.

01:22:03.320 --> 01:22:07.737
In the first edition, the
word he used was Gemeinsam

01:22:09.090 --> 01:22:12.323
and for Gemeinsam, common
is a proper translation.

01:22:13.380 --> 01:22:18.380
In the second edition, Marx
used the word Gemeinschaft leash

01:22:20.980 --> 01:22:24.183
and here, common is a
very poor translation.

01:22:24.183 --> 01:22:28.040
Gemeinschaft leash comes from the noun

01:22:28.040 --> 01:22:30.823
Gemeinschaft, community.

01:22:32.193 --> 01:22:35.780
Gemeinschaft leash, you have something

01:22:35.780 --> 01:22:38.323
when the community has it.

01:22:39.360 --> 01:22:44.360
So, that Marx changed
from Gemeinsam, common,

01:22:46.240 --> 01:22:50.690
to gemeinschaft leash in community for me

01:22:50.690 --> 01:22:55.390
is very clear sign that he saw

01:22:55.390 --> 01:22:58.730
the value substance as something

01:22:58.730 --> 01:23:03.730
which is only existing when there is

01:23:03.730 --> 01:23:07.700
a relation between the two commodities

01:23:07.700 --> 01:23:12.223
and this relation is in
the exchange relation.

01:23:13.170 --> 01:23:18.170
In the exchange relation,
we can say the one commodity

01:23:18.180 --> 01:23:21.230
has a magnitude of value
and the other commodity

01:23:21.230 --> 01:23:23.530
has a magnitude of value.

01:23:23.530 --> 01:23:28.310
With this I agree with Fred,

01:23:28.310 --> 01:23:32.060
but outside this relation,

01:23:32.060 --> 01:23:35.793
you cannot speak about
magnitudes of value.

01:23:36.760 --> 01:23:40.237
So, with this I want also to close

01:23:40.237 --> 01:23:45.237
my answer to Fred, thank you very much.

01:23:46.099 --> 01:23:47.640
- Thank you, Michael.

01:23:47.640 --> 01:23:49.210
We appreciate your coming back.

01:23:49.210 --> 01:23:51.250
And your being such a good support

01:23:51.250 --> 01:23:53.980
and giving us a lot to think about.

01:23:53.980 --> 01:23:56.200
It's great when we have family fights,

01:23:56.200 --> 01:24:00.040
it's great when hermeneutic
questions get opened up.

01:24:00.040 --> 01:24:03.460
It's always interesting to me when people

01:24:03.460 --> 01:24:08.460
use the the hagiographical move,

01:24:09.030 --> 01:24:10.341
which is this is what Marx was saying.

01:24:10.341 --> 01:24:12.000
And when people use the move,

01:24:12.000 --> 01:24:16.500
well, even if he was saying
that he might not be right.

01:24:16.500 --> 01:24:20.080
There's these ways of looking
at the text are different

01:24:20.080 --> 01:24:21.140
and they're both important

01:24:21.140 --> 01:24:25.000
and they move around in
different people's readings.

01:24:25.000 --> 01:24:27.463
Fred, I'm gonna give the
microphone over to you.

01:24:28.320 --> 01:24:29.513
Do you want to respond?

01:24:31.530 --> 01:24:34.533
- A few responses I also
wanna hear from other people.

01:24:35.886 --> 01:24:40.713
So let's start with the first point about

01:24:45.426 --> 01:24:50.426
the magnitude of value cannot
be known in production.

01:24:53.120 --> 01:24:58.120
And I'm not arguing that the magnitude

01:24:59.340 --> 01:25:01.482
of value social necessary labor time.

01:25:01.482 --> 01:25:04.950
It can be known in production.

01:25:04.950 --> 01:25:09.950
I argue that Marx assumes
that it exist in production

01:25:10.970 --> 01:25:12.509
in a system of commodity production,

01:25:12.509 --> 01:25:15.933
we're not, so it's produced for exchange.

01:25:17.010 --> 01:25:17.843
Right?

01:25:17.843 --> 01:25:19.100
So it's already in production,

01:25:19.100 --> 01:25:20.834
it's in an exchange relationship.

01:25:20.834 --> 01:25:25.750
It's in an economy based on
commodity exchange, right?

01:25:25.750 --> 01:25:28.110
So I'm certainly not saying that

01:25:29.180 --> 01:25:31.960
it is known or has to be known, right?

01:25:31.960 --> 01:25:34.430
I mean, it exists.

01:25:34.430 --> 01:25:36.650
And my point is that I stressed

01:25:36.650 --> 01:25:38.870
that section three is about,

01:25:38.870 --> 01:25:43.250
it's not knowable in production,

01:25:43.250 --> 01:25:46.950
it's not knowable in
terms of abstract labor.

01:25:46.950 --> 01:25:51.010
That's why money is necessary, right?

01:25:51.010 --> 01:25:56.010
So I think that kind of
misses my point all together,

01:25:58.920 --> 01:26:01.440
that socially necessary labor time

01:26:03.140 --> 01:26:08.140
in order to exist, but is unknown, right?

01:26:08.750 --> 01:26:10.786
Is assumed to exist, right?

01:26:10.786 --> 01:26:14.590
And in order to become known, right?

01:26:14.590 --> 01:26:19.590
It has to express its hidden value

01:26:20.870 --> 01:26:23.800
in the observable form of money.

01:26:23.800 --> 01:26:28.800
So I think you are conflating
know and exist, right?

01:26:34.413 --> 01:26:37.310
And I'm not conflating that,

01:26:37.310 --> 01:26:40.140
I'm emphasizing that that's not the case.

01:26:40.140 --> 01:26:44.293
That value is not known until exchange,

01:26:45.200 --> 01:26:48.007
until the form of appearance, right?

01:26:49.086 --> 01:26:50.283
Just the form of appearance.

01:26:53.640 --> 01:26:57.793
I think the reference to
the USSR is not relevant.

01:27:02.960 --> 01:27:07.733
As I'm not, this is a
theory of capitalism, right?

01:27:07.733 --> 01:27:11.330
And it was attempted
to be applied in crude

01:27:11.330 --> 01:27:14.370
ways to a planned economy,

01:27:14.370 --> 01:27:15.956
but I don't think that has anything to do

01:27:15.956 --> 01:27:20.956
with the validity or lack
thereof of Marx's theory,

01:27:21.410 --> 01:27:24.813
certainly not my interpretation.

01:27:26.640 --> 01:27:31.640
And on simultaneity of
everything in capitalism,

01:27:38.040 --> 01:27:43.040
I think the body analogy
is not a good one.

01:27:45.650 --> 01:27:50.650
Because Marx is analyzing
capitalism with in,

01:27:52.510 --> 01:27:55.123
which is a process, right?

01:27:55.123 --> 01:28:00.123
And the process is the
circuit of capital, right?

01:28:00.910 --> 01:28:02.514
And the circuit of capital goes

01:28:02.514 --> 01:28:06.870
through distinct phases, right?

01:28:06.870 --> 01:28:10.580
Capital exists first on the market,

01:28:10.580 --> 01:28:14.440
in the form of money
used to purchase means

01:28:14.440 --> 01:28:15.800
of production and labor power,

01:28:15.800 --> 01:28:19.630
then capital exist in production, right?

01:28:19.630 --> 01:28:24.630
Where Marx says exclusively
a capitalist formed, right?

01:28:25.300 --> 01:28:27.700
Surplus value is produced, right?

01:28:27.700 --> 01:28:29.900
And then, right?

01:28:29.900 --> 01:28:34.511
Consecutively in time, there is the return

01:28:34.511 --> 01:28:37.924
to the market and the sale of the markets

01:28:37.924 --> 01:28:42.490
of the products produced
and the realization

01:28:42.490 --> 01:28:45.323
of the value produced, right?

01:28:45.323 --> 01:28:50.323
I'm not saying that value is
known in production, right?

01:28:52.190 --> 01:28:54.400
I'm saying value is produced.

01:28:54.400 --> 01:28:57.510
Production and then subsequently,

01:28:57.510 --> 01:29:01.270
I mean, I'm not saying that simultaneous

01:29:01.270 --> 01:29:03.109
determination is not possible

01:29:03.109 --> 01:29:08.109
in other cases, I'm saying
that exchange value, right?

01:29:10.090 --> 01:29:12.600
Is determined by production.

01:29:12.600 --> 01:29:17.600
Now, in that sequence, production
comes before sale, right?

01:29:18.600 --> 01:29:22.820
And so, in that particular case,

01:29:22.820 --> 01:29:24.780
in that particular reality,

01:29:24.780 --> 01:29:29.780
there is temporal sequence
of causation, right?

01:29:30.820 --> 01:29:34.943
Because we're talking about
a process, (indistinct)

01:29:36.870 --> 01:29:39.320
So I think I'll stop there for now

01:29:39.320 --> 01:29:40.900
and maybe come back,

01:29:40.900 --> 01:29:45.120
but I like to hear from other people too.

01:29:45.120 --> 01:29:45.953
- Right.

01:29:45.953 --> 01:29:49.490
So why don't we open up
the chat for questions

01:29:49.490 --> 01:29:51.720
and maybe we can come back around

01:29:51.720 --> 01:29:55.900
to these distinctions through people's,

01:29:55.900 --> 01:29:57.190
other people's minds.

01:29:57.190 --> 01:29:58.910
There might have been
some comments earlier on

01:29:58.910 --> 01:30:01.400
but if you wanna make a comment,

01:30:01.400 --> 01:30:03.020
would you put it back in?

01:30:03.020 --> 01:30:05.933
Now repost and also post your questions.

01:30:23.150 --> 01:30:26.083
Okay, could we go to
Manuel Casique Herrera.

01:30:27.890 --> 01:30:31.063
Audrey would you open Manuel's mic?

01:30:36.980 --> 01:30:40.363
Manuel, if you're there,
you could ask your question.

01:30:45.800 --> 01:30:46.703
Do we have you?

01:30:48.390 --> 01:30:49.223
- [Manuel] Yeah.

01:30:49.223 --> 01:30:50.056
Can you hear me?

01:30:50.056 --> 01:30:50.889
- [Paul] Yes.

01:30:55.294 --> 01:30:57.060
- [Manuel] Having to speak in English

01:30:57.060 --> 01:30:59.530
in front of a whole audience.

01:30:59.530 --> 01:31:04.293
So I'm gonna try really
hard to do my best.

01:31:08.990 --> 01:31:12.043
My question is like,

01:31:13.690 --> 01:31:18.690
is obvious that market
prices have oscillations

01:31:25.274 --> 01:31:26.107
and those oscillations
if after private labor

01:31:34.710 --> 01:31:39.710
is the substance of all the value produce

01:31:44.230 --> 01:31:49.230
and all the surplus value
is the same remain the same.

01:31:51.010 --> 01:31:55.773
So, when market prices oscillate,

01:31:57.780 --> 01:32:00.470
capitalists can compete each other

01:32:01.320 --> 01:32:05.440
to appropriate surplus value produced

01:32:05.440 --> 01:32:07.570
by other capitalist

01:32:07.570 --> 01:32:11.600
because their surplus value pool remains

01:32:11.600 --> 01:32:15.343
always the same, despite
the prices oscillations.

01:32:18.759 --> 01:32:22.643
What would you think of that?

01:32:25.010 --> 01:32:26.000
- Thank you, Manuel

01:32:26.000 --> 01:32:29.580
was a perfect English
rendition of your question.

01:32:29.580 --> 01:32:31.143
Fred, do you want to respond?

01:32:33.410 --> 01:32:38.410
- Well, capitalist would
mean that was the subject

01:32:39.160 --> 01:32:43.740
of Volume III, that is
the subject of Volume III,

01:32:43.740 --> 01:32:46.720
the distribution of surplus value, right?

01:32:46.720 --> 01:32:51.310
And some capitalists
are able to appropriate

01:32:51.310 --> 01:32:53.413
more surplus value than others

01:32:53.413 --> 01:32:58.413
for various market power reasons, right?

01:32:58.780 --> 01:33:03.780
But that is a subsequent division

01:33:05.500 --> 01:33:09.833
of the surplus value that
is produced by workers.

01:33:11.250 --> 01:33:15.770
So it's a part of the theory,

01:33:15.770 --> 01:33:17.094
it can be incorporated in a theory,

01:33:17.094 --> 01:33:22.094
but because one capitalist appropriates

01:33:23.630 --> 01:33:26.840
more surplus value that another capitalist

01:33:26.840 --> 01:33:29.673
doesn't mean the capitalist
produced that surplus value.

01:33:32.040 --> 01:33:35.790
The total surplus value
comes from the surplus

01:33:35.790 --> 01:33:36.630
labor of workers,

01:33:36.630 --> 01:33:40.053
and then that's divided up
in various competitive ways.

01:33:41.957 --> 01:33:42.857
I hope that helps.

01:33:44.090 --> 01:33:46.320
- Okay, we have a question from Bilal.

01:33:46.320 --> 01:33:48.673
Can we open the Bilal's mic?

01:33:51.410 --> 01:33:55.070
Bilal (indistinct)
undergraduate in the seminar.

01:33:55.070 --> 01:33:56.960
Welcome.

01:33:56.960 --> 01:33:57.968
- [Bilal] Thank you.

01:33:57.968 --> 01:34:00.440
Thank you, Professor Moseley
for your interesting comments.

01:34:00.440 --> 01:34:03.170
I had a question about
inflation in particular,

01:34:03.170 --> 01:34:05.670
you mentioned how Marx's
theory explains the inner

01:34:05.670 --> 01:34:08.237
laws of capitalism and
not minor fluctuations.

01:34:08.237 --> 01:34:11.020
But one of the inner laws
is the sustained increase

01:34:11.020 --> 01:34:12.365
in gender price level that we've seen,

01:34:12.365 --> 01:34:14.400
constantly increasing inflation.

01:34:14.400 --> 01:34:17.660
So I was wondering whether
your interpretation of Marx

01:34:17.660 --> 01:34:19.340
maybe just Marx's writings itself,

01:34:19.340 --> 01:34:20.679
sort of tie in and can explain

01:34:20.679 --> 01:34:22.780
this in a law of capitalism.

01:34:22.780 --> 01:34:23.902
Or is it something that I don't know,

01:34:23.902 --> 01:34:25.533
a lot of Marxist theory that I've seen

01:34:25.533 --> 01:34:27.660
just somehow overlooks inflation.

01:34:27.660 --> 01:34:30.160
I'm not really sure how
Marx explains a lot of it.

01:34:33.270 --> 01:34:38.270
- Well, that's a good kind
of contemporary question.

01:34:38.336 --> 01:34:39.780
Obviously.

01:34:39.780 --> 01:34:44.080
And, in Marx's theory, in "Capital,"

01:34:47.520 --> 01:34:52.120
he assumes that money
is a commodity, right?

01:34:52.120 --> 01:34:55.020
Some people argue money
has to be a commodity,

01:34:55.020 --> 01:34:58.730
and I don't think that's true, right?

01:34:58.730 --> 01:35:03.170
But Marx's theory has to be, I think,

01:35:03.170 --> 01:35:08.170
updated and elaborated
to take into account

01:35:09.660 --> 01:35:14.660
the fact that money is no
longer a commodity like gold.

01:35:18.070 --> 01:35:19.060
Right?

01:35:19.060 --> 01:35:23.283
So I think that's an
important current task

01:35:25.500 --> 01:35:30.440
of Marxian economics is to develop

01:35:30.440 --> 01:35:35.440
a more complete theory
of non-commodity money.

01:35:36.450 --> 01:35:39.287
I've done some work on that.

01:35:39.287 --> 01:35:43.313
And I don't think it's the final answer.

01:35:44.390 --> 01:35:48.410
But based on (indistinct)

01:35:48.410 --> 01:35:50.940
when there was inconvertible,

01:35:53.680 --> 01:35:58.680
fiat money in the late 18th
and early 20th century,

01:36:00.480 --> 01:36:02.930
then, if too much money

01:36:02.930 --> 01:36:07.930
is forced into circulation,
then prices will rise, right?

01:36:11.910 --> 01:36:15.980
So that I think is at
least a starting point,

01:36:15.980 --> 01:36:19.290
it's not the total answer.

01:36:19.290 --> 01:36:24.290
And it requires much more development,

01:36:25.390 --> 01:36:30.097
but also a change of the basic function

01:36:31.810 --> 01:36:36.810
of definition of what money
is in capitalism today.

01:36:41.900 --> 01:36:42.733
- Okay, thank you.

01:36:42.733 --> 01:36:44.418
I'm going to read a question

01:36:44.418 --> 01:36:47.213
from the chat from Andy Summers.

01:36:48.610 --> 01:36:50.229
Is the disagreement in forming

01:36:50.229 --> 01:36:52.210
the surplus value problem

01:36:52.210 --> 01:36:56.690
actually related to appearance about

01:36:56.690 --> 01:36:59.710
the nature of physical
labor versus cognitive labor

01:36:59.710 --> 01:37:01.550
in the formation of a commodity socially

01:37:01.550 --> 01:37:04.043
necessary labor time and
hence, its labor power.

01:37:04.043 --> 01:37:07.253
Does value form analysis
solve that problem?

01:37:09.610 --> 01:37:10.783
Also, doesn't this also resemble

01:37:10.783 --> 01:37:14.450
the later historical
issue of monopoly capital

01:37:14.450 --> 01:37:18.440
unaddressed by Marx, but
taken on by Baran and Sweezy.

01:37:18.440 --> 01:37:19.980
Doesn't this also allow for the kinds

01:37:19.980 --> 01:37:21.563
of issues brought up by analytic Marxism

01:37:21.563 --> 01:37:24.700
in terms of inequality of distribution.

01:37:24.700 --> 01:37:25.960
Referring to Ruma.

01:37:26.940 --> 01:37:31.820
So the first question is about

01:37:31.820 --> 01:37:36.300
the difference between
physical and cognitive labor

01:37:36.300 --> 01:37:39.850
in I guess the calculation
of the magnitude

01:37:39.850 --> 01:37:43.891
or the creation of the commodities value,

01:37:43.891 --> 01:37:45.443
and the second is about.

01:37:48.374 --> 01:37:49.207
- Monopoly capital.

01:37:49.207 --> 01:37:50.470
- [Paul] Monopoly capital.

01:37:54.050 --> 01:37:58.210
- Well, I think the question
about monopoly capitalists,

01:37:58.210 --> 01:38:00.143
in my view, much easier to answer.

01:38:02.600 --> 01:38:06.790
And again, I think that was
related to the previous question

01:38:06.790 --> 01:38:11.790
that monopoly affects the
distribution of surplus, okay,

01:38:14.380 --> 01:38:18.260
in my view, interpretation
of Marxist theory,

01:38:18.260 --> 01:38:22.310
monopolies affect the
distribution of surplus value.

01:38:22.310 --> 01:38:27.250
But unless that's affecting
wages in some way,

01:38:27.250 --> 01:38:30.390
does not affect the production

01:38:30.390 --> 01:38:33.240
of surplus value and the total amount

01:38:33.240 --> 01:38:36.110
of surplus value that's produced, right?

01:38:36.110 --> 01:38:41.110
So, this I would disagree
with Baran and Sweezy, right?

01:38:42.150 --> 01:38:44.840
They thought monopoly capital actually

01:38:44.840 --> 01:38:48.410
increase surplus value, right?

01:38:48.410 --> 01:38:52.167
But I think a more
consistent interpretation

01:38:52.167 --> 01:38:57.167
would be that monopolies
get a bigger share

01:38:57.200 --> 01:38:59.260
of the total surplus value,

01:38:59.260 --> 01:39:04.260
but in general, except
maybe on a smaller scale,

01:39:04.930 --> 01:39:07.543
do not affect the production.

01:39:09.533 --> 01:39:10.680
And the total amount.

01:39:10.680 --> 01:39:12.361
- Over time as well monopolies

01:39:12.361 --> 01:39:16.660
have to cede their monopoly
at a certain point.

01:39:16.660 --> 01:39:17.493
Right?

01:39:17.493 --> 01:39:20.563
It's a temporarily
bounded problem monopoly.

01:39:21.550 --> 01:39:25.893
- Yeah, it's one aspect
of the competition.

01:39:27.570 --> 01:39:30.643
And competition has to do with,

01:39:35.170 --> 01:39:38.540
determines the distribution
of surplus value,

01:39:38.540 --> 01:39:41.153
and that will certainly change over time.

01:39:43.890 --> 01:39:46.723
The cognitive question, I
think, is another challenge.

01:39:50.562 --> 01:39:51.807
I think obviously is value producing

01:40:00.650 --> 01:40:05.650
so I mean, this would be one
one example of skilled labor.

01:40:07.928 --> 01:40:11.806
And so the cognitive labor would

01:40:11.806 --> 01:40:16.420
produce in an hour more value.

01:40:16.420 --> 01:40:18.100
How much more value?

01:40:18.100 --> 01:40:18.950
We don't know.

01:40:21.600 --> 01:40:26.600
But that is what Marx's
theory would suggest.

01:40:32.976 --> 01:40:35.280
Then the question is how

01:40:39.790 --> 01:40:42.467
important is it to know

01:40:42.467 --> 01:40:46.213
these reduction coefficients?

01:40:47.660 --> 01:40:49.870
And I think it is of some importance,

01:40:49.870 --> 01:40:52.130
but I mean, Marx said at
the end of chapter seven,

01:40:52.130 --> 01:40:53.700
his theory of surplus value,

01:40:53.700 --> 01:40:57.950
even though he didn't present any theory

01:40:57.950 --> 01:41:02.950
of what determines those
reduction coefficient,

01:41:03.010 --> 01:41:05.410
so to speak, instead, the same theory

01:41:05.410 --> 01:41:08.170
applies to skilling, right?

01:41:08.170 --> 01:41:11.730
That skilled labor is paid more, right?

01:41:11.730 --> 01:41:16.403
But they're also produce
value at a higher rate.

01:41:19.127 --> 01:41:20.651
And therefore, the basic theory

01:41:20.651 --> 01:41:23.370
of surplus value still applies.

01:41:23.370 --> 01:41:26.993
And I think that would
also apply to cognitive.

01:41:30.530 --> 01:41:32.928
- Okay, I wanna invite anyone else

01:41:32.928 --> 01:41:36.360
in the listenership, watchers, viewership

01:41:36.360 --> 01:41:38.393
to pose some questions.

01:41:39.400 --> 01:41:41.070
While you're doing that, I wanna

01:41:41.070 --> 01:41:43.459
go back to something
that Michael brought up.

01:41:43.459 --> 01:41:46.870
That needs a lot more exposition.

01:41:46.870 --> 01:41:48.480
And I know, maybe he's done it elsewhere.

01:41:48.480 --> 01:41:53.440
But I wanna ask you Fred
about his proposition that,

01:41:53.440 --> 01:41:57.080
in fact, exchange enters
into the constitution

01:41:57.080 --> 01:41:59.870
of socially necessary labor time.

01:41:59.870 --> 01:42:02.850
That is, products that are produced

01:42:02.850 --> 01:42:04.176
and not realized in exchange

01:42:04.176 --> 01:42:05.733
don't get counted in the average.

01:42:05.733 --> 01:42:07.590
So this is a backdoor way

01:42:07.590 --> 01:42:09.034
and that doesn't have anything to do

01:42:09.034 --> 01:42:11.800
with the supply demand problem.

01:42:11.800 --> 01:42:13.250
What do you think about that?

01:42:14.620 --> 01:42:17.870
- Well, I think the theory assumes

01:42:17.870 --> 01:42:22.577
a system in which what
is produced is exchanged.

01:42:23.841 --> 01:42:28.841
We're looking at a totality, in that case,

01:42:34.127 --> 01:42:38.490
the company that sells elsewhere, right?

01:42:38.490 --> 01:42:40.790
In another market, would that be included

01:42:40.790 --> 01:42:44.010
in the determination of
socially necessary labor time,

01:42:44.010 --> 01:42:48.360
is not a purely physical
thing in any country,

01:42:48.360 --> 01:42:51.081
you do a weighted average
of the labor times

01:42:51.081 --> 01:42:53.920
and determine socially
necessary labor time,

01:42:53.920 --> 01:42:56.653
is goods that were
produced for this market.

01:42:57.838 --> 01:43:02.838
And that they're the only
ones that go into the average.

01:43:04.910 --> 01:43:06.153
I don't see a problem.

01:43:08.830 --> 01:43:11.730
- Looks like there aren't
any other questions.

01:43:11.730 --> 01:43:13.396
We wanna thank you for your presentation.

01:43:13.396 --> 01:43:17.200
Invite you to not sign off
while we let everyone else go.

01:43:17.200 --> 01:43:19.000
Let them thank you for coming

01:43:19.000 --> 01:43:21.560
and presenting really important material.

01:43:21.560 --> 01:43:22.393
So thank you.

01:43:23.868 --> 01:43:24.868
- Thank you.

01:43:27.040 --> 01:43:31.043
- Thank you also, it was
nice to meet Fred again.

01:43:31.043 --> 01:43:32.630
(Fred laughs)

01:43:32.630 --> 01:43:33.910
- Thanks for your comments as always,

01:43:33.910 --> 01:43:36.590
and we will be in touch I'm sure.

01:43:36.590 --> 01:43:37.442
- [Michael] Yeah.

01:43:37.442 --> 01:43:38.275
(Fred laughs)

01:43:38.275 --> 01:43:39.397
- Thank you, Michael.

